[Event "Chapter 1: Staunton Gambit / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2939589f-927f-4210-a61e-b3bb1fd178ee/studies/fa290f1f-99db-4ee3-90b5-f0af1ccf879d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our Chapter on the Staunton Gambit. The idea behind this gambit is to get to the Black king via the weakened h5-e8 diagonal. But Black has some precise ways of playing to keep the pawn and defend against the initiative. Let's see what they are!} 1...f5 2.e4 {But now an aggressive player with the White pieces may try to subvert our expectations! This gambit opens up the queen and undermines our control of the e4-square, seeking to attract our pawn away from its stable position. But, like I said in the introduction, I believe we are sufficiently equipped to accept this gambit. Our pawn does control some squares, and ...d5 can be played at the right time.} 2...fxe4 3.f3 {3. f3 occurs 17% of the time. Around 118,000 times in the database. The point is that White wants quick development. They are playing in gambit fashion and material isn't important to them. I suggest we just support this pawn.} 3...d5 4.fxe4 {You'll usually see this capture so that our e-pawn becomes isolated on e4. We'll just recapture and make White have to work to recover the pawn.} 4...dxe4 5.Nc3 {They'll usually attempt 5. Nc3 to attack it. And we'll just defend.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {And when White develops their bishop with the intent of removing the defender, we actually don't mind this. We're happy if White wants to give up their bishop pair just to take material back. In the meantime, we can develop and put pressure on d4.} 6...Nc6 7.d5 {Instead of taking on f6 right away, pushing the d-pawn is also a common idea. Luckily, our knight has a pretty good square on which to land.} 7...Ne5 8.Qd4 {If our knight is attacked, it can be moved and we'll be fine. f7 is a great square because it's safe and it attacks the g5-bishop.} 8...Nf7 9.Bxf6 {White takes. We recapture away from the center so that we can develop our dark-squared bishop.} 9...exf6 10.Qxe4+ {White could also take on e4 with check. We should block with the queen here and make sure White never gets an attack.} 10...Qe7 11.Qxe7+ {White takes. And we recapture. We have the bishop pair and a safe position.} 11...Bxe7 12.Nb5 {If the knight goes after c7...} 12...Kd8 {We'll play ...Kd8. A similar idea to some of the things we saw in the 3. Nc3 line.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Staunton Gambit / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2939589f-927f-4210-a61e-b3bb1fd178ee/studies/fa290f1f-99db-4ee3-90b5-f0af1ccf879d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our Chapter on the Staunton Gambit. The idea behind this gambit is to get to the Black king via the weakened h5-e8 diagonal. But Black has some precise ways of playing to keep the pawn and defend against the initiative. Let's see what they are!} 1...f5 2.e4 {But now an aggressive player with the White pieces may try to subvert our expectations! This gambit opens up the queen and undermines our control of the e4-square, seeking to attract our pawn away from its stable position. But, like I said in the introduction, I believe we are sufficiently equipped to accept this gambit. Our pawn does control some squares, and ...d5 can be played at the right time.} 2...fxe4 3.Nc3 {At the master and amateur level, the move 3. Nc3 is the most popular. The idea is immediate pressure on e4. The best move here is to defend the pawn with the knight. Recall I said we'd play ...d5 at the right time. Move 3 isn't the right time because White had Qh5+, piling up another attacker on d5.} 3...Nf6 4.Bg5 {Once again, both amateurs and masters agree that trying to remove the pawn's defender is best. I really like 4...Nc6 here. My idea is that we focus on development. If White wants to give us the bishop pair, we'll be comfortable with that, but we cannot fall back in development.} 4...Nc6 5.d5 {d5 is now the most popular, seeking to dislodge our knight. But e5 is our go-to square, safe and only capable of being targeted with moves like Qd4.} 5...Ne5 6.Qd4 {The queen can come to d4. This is the most popular idea and attacks our knight. 6. Bxf6 would tranpose to 5. Bxf6 with d5. Here I recommend we fall back and attack the bishop. No other square is safe or makes sense.} 6...Nf7 7.Bxf6 {You could reasonably see this capture played now. Recapturing away from the center is key for development.} 7...exf6 8.Qxe4+ {Like I said, this isn't the most common nor is it what you should expect. Nevertheless, you could face it and it's instructive to see why White shouldn't play it.} 8...Qe7 9.Qxe7+ {Simply stated, the trade of the queens results in a simplified position that Black has no trouble defending and White's anti-positional play (giving up the bishop pair, for instance) does not get any dynamic rewards. We recapture and develop.} 9...Bxe7 10.Nb5 {The knight attacks c7.} 10...Kd8 {And our king holds everything together. We're in the endgame now, so not castling isn't a big deal. We'll go ...Bc5 and ...a6 to kick the knight out and we'll have a slight edge thanks to our strong dark-squared bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Staunton Gambit / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2939589f-927f-4210-a61e-b3bb1fd178ee/studies/fa290f1f-99db-4ee3-90b5-f0af1ccf879d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our Chapter on the Staunton Gambit. The idea behind this gambit is to get to the Black king via the weakened h5-e8 diagonal. But Black has some precise ways of playing to keep the pawn and defend against the initiative. Let's see what they are!} 1...f5 2.e4 {But now an aggressive player with the White pieces may try to subvert our expectations! This gambit opens up the queen and undermines our control of the e4-square, seeking to attract our pawn away from its stable position. But, like I said in the introduction, I believe we are sufficiently equipped to accept this gambit. Our pawn does control some squares, and ...d5 can be played at the right time.} 2...fxe4 3.Nc3 {At the master and amateur level, the move 3. Nc3 is the most popular. The idea is immediate pressure on e4. The best move here is to defend the pawn with the knight. Recall I said we'd play ...d5 at the right time. Move 3 isn't the right time because White had Qh5+, piling up another attacker on d5.} 3...Nf6 4.Bg5 {Once again, both amateurs and masters agree that trying to remove the pawn's defender is best. I really like 4...Nc6 here. My idea is that we focus on development. If White wants to give us the bishop pair, we'll be comfortable with that, but we cannot fall back in development.} 4...Nc6 5.d5 {d5 is now the most popular, seeking to dislodge our knight. But e5 is our go-to square, safe and only capable of being targeted with moves like Qd4.} 5...Ne5 6.Qd4 {The queen can come to d4. This is the most popular idea and attacks our knight. 6. Bxf6 would tranpose to 5. Bxf6 with d5. Here I recommend we fall back and attack the bishop. No other square is safe or makes sense.} 6...Nf7 7.h4 {7. h4 is an interesting move and the second most popular here. But we'll go 7...c6 with the main idea of playing 8...Qb6 and proposing a queen trade. In all likelihood, White won't accept this queen trade and Black still has to untangle.} 7...c6 8.O-O-O {For example if castling... We propose a trade. If White trades, we are happy. But if the queen goes to d2, we should trade pieces with 9...Nxd5, 10. Nxd5 cxd5 11. Qxd5 and go 11...Qc6. The benefit of trading on d5 is that we'll be able to go ...e6 to develop the bishop. The more trades we get in, the less of an attacking game White will have.} 8...Qb6 9.Qd2 {Let's see how this would look. We trade.} 9...Nxd5 10.Nxd5 {White takes. We recapture.} 10...cxd5 11.Qxd5 {After White grabs in the center... We propose a trade and x-ray our e-pawn.} 11...Qc6 12.Qd2 {If White goes back...} 12...e6 {Trading pieces has really helped us diminish White's pressure. We'll develop with ...Bc5, ...b5 and ...Bb7.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Staunton Gambit / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2939589f-927f-4210-a61e-b3bb1fd178ee/studies/fa290f1f-99db-4ee3-90b5-f0af1ccf879d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our Chapter on the Staunton Gambit. The idea behind this gambit is to get to the Black king via the weakened h5-e8 diagonal. But Black has some precise ways of playing to keep the pawn and defend against the initiative. Let's see what they are!} 1...f5 2.e4 {But now an aggressive player with the White pieces may try to subvert our expectations! This gambit opens up the queen and undermines our control of the e4-square, seeking to attract our pawn away from its stable position. But, like I said in the introduction, I believe we are sufficiently equipped to accept this gambit. Our pawn does control some squares, and ...d5 can be played at the right time.} 2...fxe4 3.f3 {3. f3 occurs 17% of the time. Around 118,000 times in the database. The point is that White wants quick development. They are playing in gambit fashion and material isn't important to them. I suggest we just support this pawn.} 3...d5 4.fxe4 {You'll usually see this capture so that our e-pawn becomes isolated on e4. We'll just recapture and make White have to work to recover the pawn.} 4...dxe4 5.Nc3 {They'll usually attempt 5. Nc3 to attack it. And we'll just defend.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {And when White develops their bishop with the intent of removing the defender, we actually don't mind this. We're happy if White wants to give up their bishop pair just to take material back. In the meantime, we can develop and put pressure on d4.} 6...Nc6 7.Bxf6 {Most likely what you will see is that White will capture the defender. Now, very important! We capture away from the center. We don't want to allow Qh5+ ideas.} 7...exf6 8.Nxe4 {We've seen some lines with 8. Nxe4. Whenever an enemy piece aligns with their king, we have to be on the lookout for pins. 8...Qe7 wins here, easily.} 8...Qe7 9.Bd3 {Defending the knight doesn't work.} 9...f5 {Because the pin isn't removed. Black wins.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Staunton Gambit / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2939589f-927f-4210-a61e-b3bb1fd178ee/studies/fa290f1f-99db-4ee3-90b5-f0af1ccf879d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our Chapter on the Staunton Gambit. The idea behind this gambit is to get to the Black king via the weakened h5-e8 diagonal. But Black has some precise ways of playing to keep the pawn and defend against the initiative. Let's see what they are!} 1...f5 2.e4 {But now an aggressive player with the White pieces may try to subvert our expectations! This gambit opens up the queen and undermines our control of the e4-square, seeking to attract our pawn away from its stable position. But, like I said in the introduction, I believe we are sufficiently equipped to accept this gambit. Our pawn does control some squares, and ...d5 can be played at the right time.} 2...fxe4 3.f3 {3. f3 occurs 17% of the time. Around 118,000 times in the database. The point is that White wants quick development. They are playing in gambit fashion and material isn't important to them. I suggest we just support this pawn.} 3...d5 4.fxe4 {You'll usually see this capture so that our e-pawn becomes isolated on e4. We'll just recapture and make White have to work to recover the pawn.} 4...dxe4 5.Nc3 {They'll usually attempt 5. Nc3 to attack it. And we'll just defend.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {And when White develops their bishop with the intent of removing the defender, we actually don't mind this. We're happy if White wants to give up their bishop pair just to take material back. In the meantime, we can develop and put pressure on d4.} 6...Nc6 7.Bxf6 {Most likely what you will see is that White will capture the defender. Now, very important! We capture away from the center. We don't want to allow Qh5+ ideas.} 7...exf6 8.Nxe4 {We've seen some lines with 8. Nxe4. Whenever an enemy piece aligns with their king, we have to be on the lookout for pins. 8...Qe7 wins here, easily.} 8...Qe7 9.Qe2 {It seems White can break the pin.} 9...Nxd4 {But the defense is illusory. 10. Qe3 falls to ...Nxc2+. 10. Qd3 fails to moves like ...c5 or ...Qb4, attacking b2.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Staunton Gambit / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2939589f-927f-4210-a61e-b3bb1fd178ee/studies/fa290f1f-99db-4ee3-90b5-f0af1ccf879d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our Chapter on the Staunton Gambit. The idea behind this gambit is to get to the Black king via the weakened h5-e8 diagonal. But Black has some precise ways of playing to keep the pawn and defend against the initiative. Let's see what they are!} 1...f5 2.e4 {But now an aggressive player with the White pieces may try to subvert our expectations! This gambit opens up the queen and undermines our control of the e4-square, seeking to attract our pawn away from its stable position. But, like I said in the introduction, I believe we are sufficiently equipped to accept this gambit. Our pawn does control some squares, and ...d5 can be played at the right time.} 2...fxe4 3.Qh5+ {White could also attemp to be trickier by giving this check. We'll just block it.} 3...g6 4.Be2 {If 4. Be2, don't take the queen on h5! The diagonal is weak and you will be checkmated! Instead, go for smart development and attack a pawn while covering the e5-square.} 4...Nc6 5.d5 {White may think that with 5. d5 they're forcing you to move the knight. They may think 5...Nd4 and 6. Qe5, double attack! But this tricky little trap attacks the d5-pawn and threatens the queen.} 5...e6 6.dxc6 {If White takes on c6... We can take the queen because the threat of checkmate has disappeared.} 6...gxh5 7.Bxh5+ {Against this check, we can move the king. Our king is safe on e7.} 7...Ke7 8.Bg5+ {And while White does have another check, it won't achieve much.} 8...Nf6 {We can block the check and get ready to defend our knight further next.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Staunton Gambit / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2939589f-927f-4210-a61e-b3bb1fd178ee/studies/fa290f1f-99db-4ee3-90b5-f0af1ccf879d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our Chapter on the Staunton Gambit. The idea behind this gambit is to get to the Black king via the weakened h5-e8 diagonal. But Black has some precise ways of playing to keep the pawn and defend against the initiative. Let's see what they are!} 1...f5 2.e4 {But now an aggressive player with the White pieces may try to subvert our expectations! This gambit opens up the queen and undermines our control of the e4-square, seeking to attract our pawn away from its stable position. But, like I said in the introduction, I believe we are sufficiently equipped to accept this gambit. Our pawn does control some squares, and ...d5 can be played at the right time.} 2...fxe4 3.f3 {3. f3 occurs 17% of the time. Around 118,000 times in the database. The point is that White wants quick development. They are playing in gambit fashion and material isn't important to them. I suggest we just support this pawn.} 3...d5 4.fxe4 {You'll usually see this capture so that our e-pawn becomes isolated on e4. We'll just recapture and make White have to work to recover the pawn.} 4...dxe4 5.Nc3 {They'll usually attempt 5. Nc3 to attack it. And we'll just defend.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {And when White develops their bishop with the intent of removing the defender, we actually don't mind this. We're happy if White wants to give up their bishop pair just to take material back. In the meantime, we can develop and put pressure on d4.} 6...Nc6 7.d5 {Instead of taking on f6 right away, pushing the d-pawn is also a common idea. Luckily, our knight has a pretty good square on which to land.} 7...Ne5 8.Qd4 {If our knight is attacked, it can be moved and we'll be fine. f7 is a great square because it's safe and it attacks the g5-bishop.} 8...Nf7 9.Bxf6 {White takes. We recapture away from the center so that we can develop our dark-squared bishop.} 9...exf6 10.Nxe4 {You may see the capture with the knight. And now I like 10...f5 to kick the knight out of the center. The idea behind our moves is that we give the pawn back, sure, but White doesn't have an attack.} 10...f5 11.Ng3 {The very natural 11. Ng3 isn't what I'd call ideal placement for the knight. Just like in the main line, ...g6 will help us develop our bishop with tempi and castle quickly. It's worth noting that 12. Qe3+ don't help White. We can block with the engine approved 12...Qe7 or 12...Be7.} 11...g6 12.O-O-O {After castling... We have a check.} 12...Bh6+ 13.Kb1 {The king moves away.} 13...O-O {And now we castle. The knight on g3 is useless for White. It doesn't have any good squares. We control e4 and e2 cannot be used because White needs to develop through that square. Because of our strong bishop coming to g7, we can even launch a pawn storm on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Staunton Gambit / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2939589f-927f-4210-a61e-b3bb1fd178ee/studies/fa290f1f-99db-4ee3-90b5-f0af1ccf879d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our Chapter on the Staunton Gambit. The idea behind this gambit is to get to the Black king via the weakened h5-e8 diagonal. But Black has some precise ways of playing to keep the pawn and defend against the initiative. Let's see what they are!} 1...f5 2.e4 {But now an aggressive player with the White pieces may try to subvert our expectations! This gambit opens up the queen and undermines our control of the e4-square, seeking to attract our pawn away from its stable position. But, like I said in the introduction, I believe we are sufficiently equipped to accept this gambit. Our pawn does control some squares, and ...d5 can be played at the right time.} 2...fxe4 3.Nc3 {At the master and amateur level, the move 3. Nc3 is the most popular. The idea is immediate pressure on e4. The best move here is to defend the pawn with the knight. Recall I said we'd play ...d5 at the right time. Move 3 isn't the right time because White had Qh5+, piling up another attacker on d5.} 3...Nf6 4.Bg5 {Once again, both amateurs and masters agree that trying to remove the pawn's defender is best. I really like 4...Nc6 here. My idea is that we focus on development. If White wants to give us the bishop pair, we'll be comfortable with that, but we cannot fall back in development.} 4...Nc6 5.d5 {d5 is now the most popular, seeking to dislodge our knight. But e5 is our go-to square, safe and only capable of being targeted with moves like Qd4.} 5...Ne5 6.Qd4 {The queen can come to d4. This is the most popular idea and attacks our knight. 6. Bxf6 would tranpose to 5. Bxf6 with d5. Here I recommend we fall back and attack the bishop. No other square is safe or makes sense.} 6...Nf7 7.Bxf6 {You could reasonably see this capture played now. Recapturing away from the center is key for development.} 7...exf6 8.Nxe4 {This is now the most common capture of the pawn. I suppose that you'll see this more often because White doesn't want to trade queens on the e-file. That would be an anti-gambit mentality, and we've already established that White is playing a gambit. Kicking the knight out of the center and getting out pawn into ...f5 is a win for us.} 8...f5 9.Ng3 {When the knight goes to g3... We can defend the pawn because our knight on f7 holds the rook!} 9...g6 10.O-O-O {White's most popular move now allows us to get out of the center quickly. Check!} 10...Bh6+ 11.Kb1 {The king moves away.} 11...O-O {And we castle kingside. In all likelihood you'll see 12. h4 going for h5. If that's the case, Black is quicker: 12...Bg7 and 13...Qf6 to threaten checkmate on b2, getting many pieces around the king to defend as well!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Staunton Gambit / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2939589f-927f-4210-a61e-b3bb1fd178ee/studies/fa290f1f-99db-4ee3-90b5-f0af1ccf879d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our Chapter on the Staunton Gambit. The idea behind this gambit is to get to the Black king via the weakened h5-e8 diagonal. But Black has some precise ways of playing to keep the pawn and defend against the initiative. Let's see what they are!} 1...f5 2.e4 {But now an aggressive player with the White pieces may try to subvert our expectations! This gambit opens up the queen and undermines our control of the e4-square, seeking to attract our pawn away from its stable position. But, like I said in the introduction, I believe we are sufficiently equipped to accept this gambit. Our pawn does control some squares, and ...d5 can be played at the right time.} 2...fxe4 3.f3 {3. f3 occurs 17% of the time. Around 118,000 times in the database. The point is that White wants quick development. They are playing in gambit fashion and material isn't important to them. I suggest we just support this pawn.} 3...d5 4.fxe4 {You'll usually see this capture so that our e-pawn becomes isolated on e4. We'll just recapture and make White have to work to recover the pawn.} 4...dxe4 5.Nc3 {They'll usually attempt 5. Nc3 to attack it. And we'll just defend.} 5...Nf6 6.Bg5 {And when White develops their bishop with the intent of removing the defender, we actually don't mind this. We're happy if White wants to give up their bishop pair just to take material back. In the meantime, we can develop and put pressure on d4.} 6...Nc6 7.Bxf6 {Most likely what you will see is that White will capture the defender. Now, very important! We capture away from the center. We don't want to allow Qh5+ ideas.} 7...exf6 8.d5 {White could also try this move order. Here, we can go into the center right away and in this variation our knight is rather stable on e5.} 8...Ne5 9.Nxe4 {If White takes at this moment, we can see how comparatively our knight is more stable. We can gain a tempo on White's knight. This clears the way for our quick development, which is why we have a -2 advantage.} 9...Bf5 10.Ng3 {If the knight goes to g3, attacking our bishop, we won't move it away.} 10...Qd7 {We'll just defend! Development is the most important thing now. We have great pressure on White's d5-pawn, and we'll develop with normal moves like ...Bc5.} *

