[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2939589f-927f-4210-a61e-b3bb1fd178ee/studies/fa290f1f-99db-4ee3-90b5-f0af1ccf879d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our Chapter on the Staunton Gambit. The idea behind this gambit is to get to the Black king via the weakened h5-e8 diagonal. But Black has some precise ways of playing to keep the pawn and defend against the initiative. Let's see what they are!} 1...f5 2.e4 {But now an aggressive player with the White pieces may try to subvert our expectations! This gambit opens up the queen and undermines our control of the e4-square, seeking to attract our pawn away from its stable position. But, like I said in the introduction, I believe we are sufficiently equipped to accept this gambit. Our pawn does control some squares, and ...d5 can be played at the right time.} 2...fxe4 3.Nc3 {At the master and amateur level, the move 3. Nc3 is the most popular. The idea is immediate pressure on e4. The best move here is to defend the pawn with the knight. Recall I said we'd play ...d5 at the right time. Move 3 isn't the right time because White had Qh5+, piling up another attacker on d5.} 3...Nf6 4.Bg5 {Once again, both amateurs and masters agree that trying to remove the pawn's defender is best. I really like 4...Nc6 here. My idea is that we focus on development. If White wants to give us the bishop pair, we'll be comfortable with that, but we cannot fall back in development.} 4...Nc6 5.Bxf6 {This is also a popular option, going for the idea behind Bg5. The best recapture is now away from the center, opening up the e7-square.} 5...exf6 6.d5 {If d5 now... We can jump into the center to our good e5-square.} 6...Ne5 7.Nxe4 {This capture isn't ideal still.} 7...f5 {Because we can still get out ...f5 move in and control the e4-square. White hasn't achieve anything out of the opening with their gambit idea. If 8. Ng3, 8...Bc5 is strong with pressure on f2. 9. Nxf5 falls to 9...Qf6 and 0-0 with more pressure on f2. Going back to c3 is a reset.} *

