1.d4 {Welcome to our Chapter on the Staunton Gambit. The idea behind this gambit is to get to the Black king via the weakened h5-e8 diagonal. But Black has some precise ways of playing to keep the pawn and defend against the initiative. Let's see what they are!} 1...f5 2.e4 {But now an aggressive player with the White pieces may try to subvert our expectations! This gambit opens up the queen and undermines our control of the e4-square, seeking to attract our pawn away from its stable position. But, like I said in the introduction, I believe we are sufficiently equipped to accept this gambit. Our pawn does control some squares, and ...d5 can be played at the right time.} 2...fxe4 3.Nc3 {At the master and amateur level, the move 3. Nc3 is the most popular. The idea is immediate pressure on e4. The best move here is to defend the pawn with the knight. Recall I said we'd play ...d5 at the right time. Move 3 isn't the right time because White had Qh5+, piling up another attacker on d5.} 3...Nf6 4.Bg5 {Once again, both amateurs and masters agree that trying to remove the pawn's defender is best. I really like 4...Nc6 here. My idea is that we focus on development. If White wants to give us the bishop pair, we'll be comfortable with that, but we cannot fall back in development.} 4...Nc6 5.Bxf6 {This is also a popular option, going for the idea behind Bg5. The best recapture is now away from the center, opening up the e7-square.} 5...exf6 6.Nxe4 {This is impossible.} 6...Qe7 {Because White would lose a piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Staunton Gambit / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2939589f-927f-4210-a61e-b3bb1fd178ee/studies/fa290f1f-99db-4ee3-90b5-f0af1ccf879d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to our Chapter on the Staunton Gambit. The idea behind this gambit is to get to the Black king via the weakened h5-e8 diagonal. But Black has some precise ways of playing to keep the pawn and defend against the initiative. Let's see what they are!} 1...f5 2.e4 {But now an aggressive player with the White pieces may try to subvert our expectations! This gambit opens up the queen and undermines our control of the e4-square, seeking to attract our pawn away from its stable position. But, like I said in the introduction, I believe we are sufficiently equipped to accept this gambit. Our pawn does control some squares, and ...d5 can be played at the right time.} 2...fxe4 3.Qh5+ {White could also attemp to be trickier by giving this check. We'll just block it.} 3...g6 4.Qe5 {And this may be White's idea. But we can deal with the double attack by blocking the attack on the rook and defending e4.} 4...Nf6 5.Bg5 {The bishop comes into g5 and puts pressure. But our development is fast because White's queen activation was premature.} 5...Nc6 6.Qf4 {When the queen moves away...} 6...Nxd4 {We can win another pawn and should probably convert this easily!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays The London / Study 1: Obscure Lines After 2.Bf4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/0942d4f6-426e-49a6-a125-86478279dc5f/studies/687fc2c6-bb0c-4e48-b780-ca4b3f6175eb/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the London Chapter! In this chapter, we'll learn how to deal with the infamous London System, in which White develops the bishop to f4 and sets up a solid center w/ e3 & d4. Let's get started!} 1...f5 2.Bf4 {White develops the c1-bishop to f4. This is the signature square for the dark-squared bishop in the London. We'll develop our king's knight to f6 & take more control over the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.e3 {White typically proceeds with e3. This move adds support to d4 and opens up the light-squared bishop. As per usual, we'll proceed with ...e6. This move strengthens the center and opens up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.Bd3 {White can also delay developing the king's knight by starting with 4. Bd3. This is seen roughly 13% of the time. I recommend ...b6 here, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop.} 4...b6 5.Qf3 {Most of the time, White transposes to a normal London with 5. Nf3, but the rare & interesting 5. Qf3 is an option. While Qf3 might look intimidating, it is merely a one move threat on the rook. The best way to respond is ...Nc6. We develop a minor piece and defend the rook by interposing the diagonal.} 5...Nc6 6.Ne2 {White continues developing w/Ne2. Other moves are also possible, but Black's plan likely won't be affected. We'll continue by fianchettoing. The bishop is now lined up with the white queen, so we might be able to go for some discoveries in the future.} 6...Bb7 7.O-O {Most often, White castles their king.} 7...Rg8 {And I suggest we proceed with ...Rg8. Our plan is to launch a kingside pawn storm & go after White's castled king! Black is doing very well and White will have a really difficult time dealing with the oncoming kingside pawn storm.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays The London / Study 1: Obscure Lines After 2.Bf4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/0942d4f6-426e-49a6-a125-86478279dc5f/studies/687fc2c6-bb0c-4e48-b780-ca4b3f6175eb/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the London Chapter! In this chapter, we'll learn how to deal with the infamous London System, in which White develops the bishop to f4 and sets up a solid center w/ e3 & d4. Let's get started!} 1...f5 2.Bf4 {White develops the c1-bishop to f4. This is the signature square for the dark-squared bishop in the London. We'll develop our king's knight to f6 & take more control over the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.e3 {White typically proceeds with e3. This move adds support to d4 and opens up the light-squared bishop. As per usual, we'll proceed with ...e6. This move strengthens the center and opens up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.c4 {White can also grab some central space by advancing the c-pawn. 4. c4 is seen in about 6% of games. Against this, I suggest we go ...Bb4+. We are developing our dark-squared bishop with check and making use of the fact that White can't block with c3.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Nc3 {Most often, White blocks with Nc3. We'll continue by capturing the knight. We're giving up the bishop pair, but forcing White to ruin their structure.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {White recaptures.} 6...b6 {And we'll proceed with ...b6, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop. This position resembles a Gotham Dutch and the ideas are pretty similar. We'll go ...Bb7, ...O-O, ...d6 & can even jump the knight into e4 at the right moment.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays The London / Study 2: All London Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/0942d4f6-426e-49a6-a125-86478279dc5f/studies/c96b4238-a618-4010-8894-2ff4f5f1cabe/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study! Instead of playing Bf4 on the second move, White might opt to start w/Nf3. This study will dive deeper into the typical London System setup which involves Nf3 + Bf4 + e3 and Bd3. Let's take a look!} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {White develops the king's knight. This move is almost as common as the move 2. Bf4 seen in study one. We'll develop our king's knight to f6 and take more control over the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Bf4 {White develops the c1-bishop to f4. This is the signature square for the dark-squared bishop in the London. As per usual, we'll proceed with ...e6. This move strengthens the center and opens up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e3 {White almost always proceeds w/e3. This move adds support to d4 and opens up the light-squared bishop. And we'll proceed with ...b6, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop.} 4...b6 5.Bd3 {White develops the f1-bishop to d3. Other moves, such as Nbd2 or c4, are possible, but they quickly transpose. We'll continue by fianchettoing. White now has two options...} 5...Bb7 6.Nbd2 {Most often, White goes for Nbd2. This is a flexible move - White doesn't commit their king and waits for Black. We'll offer a bishop trade w/...Bd6! We're looking to trade off our dark-squared bishop, which wouldn't have great prospects on e7, for White's active bishop on f4. If White is threatening e4, we should capture the dark squared bishop.} 6...Bd6 7.Bxd6 {Most natural is to trade the bishops. This is seen about 24% of the time. Positionally speaking, White trades off their active & well-placed bishop, but Black gets doubled pawns on d6/d7. We will, of course, recapture. The d-pawns are doubled, but we have ideas to castle, play d6-d5, Nc6, and jump into the e4 square.} 7...cxd6 8.O-O {White typically castles their king. We'll also get our king to safety.} 8...O-O 9.Qe2 {White prepares to push e4 with Qe2. We'll go ...d5, clamping down on e4. We've now established a really solid central structure. If White plays slowly and doesn't attack our structure, we can plan to go ...Nc6, ...d6, ...Qd7 & ...e5!} 9...d5 10.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, looking to destabilize our solid pawn center. We'll simply develop our knight. No need to release the tension.} 10...Nc6 11.cxd5 {White releases the tension w/cxd5. And we'll recapture with the knight. Regarding some typical ideas in the early middlegame, we'll place a rook on the c-file and it's often a good idea to rotate the knights back to e7 & f6.} 11...Nxd5 12.e4 {If White rushes with the e4-break...} 12...Nf4 {We can respond with ...Nf4! White is completely toast here.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays The London / Study 2: All London Lines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/0942d4f6-426e-49a6-a125-86478279dc5f/studies/c96b4238-a618-4010-8894-2ff4f5f1cabe/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study! Instead of playing Bf4 on the second move, White might opt to start w/Nf3. This study will dive deeper into the typical London System setup which involves Nf3 + Bf4 + e3 and Bd3. Let's take a look!} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {White develops the king's knight. This move is almost as common as the move 2. Bf4 seen in study one. We'll develop our king's knight to f6 and take more control over the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Bf4 {White develops the c1-bishop to f4. This is the signature square for the dark-squared bishop in the London. As per usual, we'll proceed with ...e6. This move strengthens the center and opens up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e3 {White almost always proceeds w/e3. This move adds support to d4 and opens up the light-squared bishop. And we'll proceed with ...b6, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop.} 4...b6 5.Bd3 {White develops the f1-bishop to d3. Other moves, such as Nbd2 or c4, are possible, but they quickly transpose. We'll continue by fianchettoing. White now has two options...} 5...Bb7 6.O-O {White's other option is to castle. This is half as common as Nbd2. Against O-O, I also suggest ...Bd6. The idea is, once again, that we are looking to trade off our dark-squared bishop, which wouldn't have great prospects on e7, for the f4-bishop. White now has some options...} 6...Bd6 7.Bxd6 {Most common is to trade the bishops. This is seen about 40% of the time. We will, of course, recapture. The moment White threatens e3-e4, we advance with d6-d5. Only one move by White is unique here...} 7...cxd6 8.c4 {Which is this. White takes space and is looking to develop the knight behind the c-pawn. Against this, I suggest we go for ...d5 anyway.} 8...d5 9.cxd5 {White captures the d5-pawn. We'll recapture with the knight, so that our light squared bishop always has an open diagonal.} 9...Nxd5 10.Nc3 {White goes Nc3, offering a trade. We'll go ...Nxc3 and trade the knights.} 10...Nxc3 11.bxc3 {White recaptures. We'll continue by castling our king.} 11...O-O 12.Qe2 {White prepares to push e4 with Qe2. It's important not to overcommit by advancing the d-pawn - this would leave us with a backward e6-pawn. Instead, we'll simply develop w/...Nc6.} 12...Nc6 13.e4 {And when White pushes the e-pawn...} 13...Ne7 {We'll respond ...Ne7, supporting f5! Short-term, we'll go ...Rc8, and can go for ..Ng6, with the idea to jump into f4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays The London / Study 2: All London Lines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/0942d4f6-426e-49a6-a125-86478279dc5f/studies/c96b4238-a618-4010-8894-2ff4f5f1cabe/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study! Instead of playing Bf4 on the second move, White might opt to start w/Nf3. This study will dive deeper into the typical London System setup which involves Nf3 + Bf4 + e3 and Bd3. Let's take a look!} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {White develops the king's knight. This move is almost as common as the move 2. Bf4 seen in study one. We'll develop our king's knight to f6 and take more control over the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Bf4 {White develops the c1-bishop to f4. This is the signature square for the dark-squared bishop in the London. As per usual, we'll proceed with ...e6. This move strengthens the center and opens up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e3 {White almost always proceeds w/e3. This move adds support to d4 and opens up the light-squared bishop. And we'll proceed with ...b6, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop.} 4...b6 5.Bd3 {White develops the f1-bishop to d3. Other moves, such as Nbd2 or c4, are possible, but they quickly transpose. We'll continue by fianchettoing. White now has two options...} 5...Bb7 6.Nbd2 {Most often, White goes for Nbd2. This is a flexible move - White doesn't commit their king and waits for Black. We'll offer a bishop trade w/...Bd6! We're looking to trade off our dark-squared bishop, which wouldn't have great prospects on e7, for White's active bishop on f4. If White is threatening e4, we should capture the dark squared bishop.} 6...Bd6 7.Bg3 {7. Bg3 is also a common response. White keeps the tension between the bishops & is looking to trade on their own terms, as ...Bxg3 would allow White to recapture hxg3 and open the h-file. We'll continue by castling our king. We'll wait for White to castle & then trade, as this makes it so that White can't make use of the open h-file as there's no rook on h1.} 7...O-O 8.c3 {White proceeds in typical London style by building a triangle with c3. I suggest we proceed with ...Qe7. One of our main plans is to advance the c-pawn and follow up with ...Nc6. This plan is made possible by the fact that the queen defends the d6-bishop.} 8...Qe7 9.O-O {White gets their king to safety. Now we'll trade the bishops! Once again, we trade now because White has castled kingside and the rook has been moved off of the h-file.} 9...Bxg3 10.hxg3 {White recaptures with the h-pawn.} 10...c5 {And we'll advance our c-pawn. A complex middlegame is up ahead. We can look to follow up with ...Nc6 and increase control over e4 w/...d5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays The London / Study 2: All London Lines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/0942d4f6-426e-49a6-a125-86478279dc5f/studies/c96b4238-a618-4010-8894-2ff4f5f1cabe/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study! Instead of playing Bf4 on the second move, White might opt to start w/Nf3. This study will dive deeper into the typical London System setup which involves Nf3 + Bf4 + e3 and Bd3. Let's take a look!} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {White develops the king's knight. This move is almost as common as the move 2. Bf4 seen in study one. We'll develop our king's knight to f6 and take more control over the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Bf4 {White develops the c1-bishop to f4. This is the signature square for the dark-squared bishop in the London. As per usual, we'll proceed with ...e6. This move strengthens the center and opens up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e3 {White almost always proceeds w/e3. This move adds support to d4 and opens up the light-squared bishop. And we'll proceed with ...b6, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop.} 4...b6 5.Bd3 {White develops the f1-bishop to d3. Other moves, such as Nbd2 or c4, are possible, but they quickly transpose. We'll continue by fianchettoing. White now has two options...} 5...Bb7 6.O-O {White's other option is to castle. This is half as common as Nbd2. Against O-O, I also suggest ...Bd6. The idea is, once again, that we are looking to trade off our dark-squared bishop, which wouldn't have great prospects on e7, for the f4-bishop. White now has some options...} 6...Bd6 7.Ne5 {White might decline the trade w/Ne5. This is seen about 23% of the time and it runs into a really savage trap! We'll push ...g5 & attack the bishop! White now has two options...} 7...g5 8.Bxg5 {White opts to capture the g-pawn. We'll proceed with ...Rg8. White's bishop is attacked, but can't move due to the threats against g2!} 8...Rg8 9.f4 {White supports the bishop with f4. We'll attack the bishop again w/...h6.} 9...h6 10.Bxf6 {White has no better than to take. We'll take on g2 with our rook!} 10...Rxg2+ 11.Kh1 {White plays the only legal move, Kh1. We'll proceed by taking the bishop. The imminent threat is ...Rd2+, a discovery to win the white queen.} 11...Qxf6 12.Qh5+ {White gives a check with the queen. We respond ...Rg6+, blocking the check and checking White's king!} 12...Rg6+ 13.d5 {White has to block the diagonal w/d5. The knight was threatening to capture our rook, so we'll simply eliminate it.} 13...Bxe5 14.fxe5 {White recaptures. Our queen is under attack...} 14...Qf7 {So we'll slide it back one square. White is toast! There's no good way to defend against ...Rg1+ and ...Bxd5+.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays The London / Study 2: All London Lines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/0942d4f6-426e-49a6-a125-86478279dc5f/studies/c96b4238-a618-4010-8894-2ff4f5f1cabe/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study! Instead of playing Bf4 on the second move, White might opt to start w/Nf3. This study will dive deeper into the typical London System setup which involves Nf3 + Bf4 + e3 and Bd3. Let's take a look!} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {White develops the king's knight. This move is almost as common as the move 2. Bf4 seen in study one. We'll develop our king's knight to f6 and take more control over the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Bf4 {White develops the c1-bishop to f4. This is the signature square for the dark-squared bishop in the London. As per usual, we'll proceed with ...e6. This move strengthens the center and opens up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e3 {White almost always proceeds w/e3. This move adds support to d4 and opens up the light-squared bishop. And we'll proceed with ...b6, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop.} 4...b6 5.Bd3 {White develops the f1-bishop to d3. Other moves, such as Nbd2 or c4, are possible, but they quickly transpose. We'll continue by fianchettoing. White now has two options...} 5...Bb7 6.O-O {White's other option is to castle. This is half as common as Nbd2. Against O-O, I also suggest ...Bd6. The idea is, once again, that we are looking to trade off our dark-squared bishop, which wouldn't have great prospects on e7, for the f4-bishop. White now has some options...} 6...Bd6 7.Bg5 {White can also respond by moving the bishop into g5 & pinning the f6-knight. This is seen about 8% of the time. I suggest we attack the bishop w/...h6. White now has a decision to make...} 7...h6 8.Bxf6 {Seeing that Bh4 can be met with the aggressive ...g5, White opts for Bxf6.} 8...Qxf6 {We'll recapture with the queen. Our short-term plan in this position is to go for ...Nc6, long castling & ...g5. Long-term, we attack White's king!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays The London / Study 2: All London Lines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/0942d4f6-426e-49a6-a125-86478279dc5f/studies/c96b4238-a618-4010-8894-2ff4f5f1cabe/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study! Instead of playing Bf4 on the second move, White might opt to start w/Nf3. This study will dive deeper into the typical London System setup which involves Nf3 + Bf4 + e3 and Bd3. Let's take a look!} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {White develops the king's knight. This move is almost as common as the move 2. Bf4 seen in study one. We'll develop our king's knight to f6 and take more control over the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Bf4 {White develops the c1-bishop to f4. This is the signature square for the dark-squared bishop in the London. As per usual, we'll proceed with ...e6. This move strengthens the center and opens up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e3 {White almost always proceeds w/e3. This move adds support to d4 and opens up the light-squared bishop. And we'll proceed with ...b6, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop.} 4...b6 5.Bd3 {White develops the f1-bishop to d3. Other moves, such as Nbd2 or c4, are possible, but they quickly transpose. We'll continue by fianchettoing. White now has two options...} 5...Bb7 6.O-O {White's other option is to castle. This is half as common as Nbd2. Against O-O, I also suggest ...Bd6. The idea is, once again, that we are looking to trade off our dark-squared bishop, which wouldn't have great prospects on e7, for the f4-bishop. White now has some options...} 6...Bd6 7.Bg5 {White can also respond by moving the bishop into g5 & pinning the f6-knight. This is seen about 8% of the time. I suggest we attack the bishop w/...h6. White now has a decision to make...} 7...h6 8.Bh4 {White goes for Bh4, keeping the pin. We'll advance our g-pawn! The kingside pawn storm is coming.} 8...g5 9.Bg3 {White moves the bishop to safety. We'll proceed by trading the bishops.} 9...Bxg3 10.hxg3 {And when White recaptures... We'll start advancing our h-pawn! We want to rip apart White's kingside.} 10...h5 11.Nxg5 {White captures the "free" g-pawn. The g-pawn is irrelevant - we push h4!} 11...h4 12.gxh4 {And when White takes the h-pawn...} 12...Rxh4 {We'll simply recapture with the rook. White is in trouble! The rook, queen (via Qe7-Qg7) and bishop will soon team up to attack White's kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays The London / Study 2: All London Lines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/0942d4f6-426e-49a6-a125-86478279dc5f/studies/c96b4238-a618-4010-8894-2ff4f5f1cabe/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study! Instead of playing Bf4 on the second move, White might opt to start w/Nf3. This study will dive deeper into the typical London System setup which involves Nf3 + Bf4 + e3 and Bd3. Let's take a look!} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {White develops the king's knight. This move is almost as common as the move 2. Bf4 seen in study one. We'll develop our king's knight to f6 and take more control over the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Bf4 {White develops the c1-bishop to f4. This is the signature square for the dark-squared bishop in the London. As per usual, we'll proceed with ...e6. This move strengthens the center and opens up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e3 {White almost always proceeds w/e3. This move adds support to d4 and opens up the light-squared bishop. And we'll proceed with ...b6, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop.} 4...b6 5.Bd3 {White develops the f1-bishop to d3. Other moves, such as Nbd2 or c4, are possible, but they quickly transpose. We'll continue by fianchettoing. White now has two options...} 5...Bb7 6.O-O {White's other option is to castle. This is half as common as Nbd2. Against O-O, I also suggest ...Bd6. The idea is, once again, that we are looking to trade off our dark-squared bishop, which wouldn't have great prospects on e7, for the f4-bishop. White now has some options...} 6...Bd6 7.Bg3 {Another option for White is 7. Bg3. This is seen about 8% of the time. Bg3 is a decent option in the 6. Nbd2 variation, but here it doesn't make as much sense since White has castled. We'll proceed by trading the bishops.} 7...Bxg3 8.hxg3 {White recaptures with the h-pawn. I suggest that we advance ...h5!} 8...h5 9.Nbd2 {And when White develops w/Nbd2...} 9...Ne4 {We'll jump our knight into e4! Black has great attacking prospects. Our plan is to go ...Qf6, ...Nc6, castle long & start a kingside attack w/...g5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays The London / Study 2: All London Lines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/0942d4f6-426e-49a6-a125-86478279dc5f/studies/c96b4238-a618-4010-8894-2ff4f5f1cabe/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study! Instead of playing Bf4 on the second move, White might opt to start w/Nf3. This study will dive deeper into the typical London System setup which involves Nf3 + Bf4 + e3 and Bd3. Let's take a look!} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {White develops the king's knight. This move is almost as common as the move 2. Bf4 seen in study one. We'll develop our king's knight to f6 and take more control over the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Bf4 {White develops the c1-bishop to f4. This is the signature square for the dark-squared bishop in the London. As per usual, we'll proceed with ...e6. This move strengthens the center and opens up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e3 {White almost always proceeds w/e3. This move adds support to d4 and opens up the light-squared bishop. And we'll proceed with ...b6, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop.} 4...b6 5.Bd3 {White develops the f1-bishop to d3. Other moves, such as Nbd2 or c4, are possible, but they quickly transpose. We'll continue by fianchettoing. White now has two options...} 5...Bb7 6.O-O {White's other option is to castle. This is half as common as Nbd2. Against O-O, I also suggest ...Bd6. The idea is, once again, that we are looking to trade off our dark-squared bishop, which wouldn't have great prospects on e7, for the f4-bishop. White now has some options...} 6...Bd6 7.Ne5 {White might decline the trade w/Ne5. This is seen about 23% of the time and it runs into a really savage trap! We'll push ...g5 & attack the bishop! White now has two options...} 7...g5 8.Bg3 {Seeing that Bxg5 runs into a world of trouble after ...Rg8, White goes Bg3. We'll still go after White w/...h5!} 8...h5 9.h4 {If White tries to stop us with h4... We'll capture White's knight.} 9...Bxe5 10.Bxe5 {And when White recaptures...} 10...gxh4 {We'll take on h4, opening the g-file. Black is crushing here. The g-file is open, ...h3 is on the way, and Black's bishop is staring towards White's king.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays The London / Study 2: All London Lines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/0942d4f6-426e-49a6-a125-86478279dc5f/studies/c96b4238-a618-4010-8894-2ff4f5f1cabe/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study! Instead of playing Bf4 on the second move, White might opt to start w/Nf3. This study will dive deeper into the typical London System setup which involves Nf3 + Bf4 + e3 and Bd3. Let's take a look!} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {White develops the king's knight. This move is almost as common as the move 2. Bf4 seen in study one. We'll develop our king's knight to f6 and take more control over the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Bf4 {White develops the c1-bishop to f4. This is the signature square for the dark-squared bishop in the London. As per usual, we'll proceed with ...e6. This move strengthens the center and opens up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e3 {White almost always proceeds w/e3. This move adds support to d4 and opens up the light-squared bishop. And we'll proceed with ...b6, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop.} 4...b6 5.Bd3 {White develops the f1-bishop to d3. Other moves, such as Nbd2 or c4, are possible, but they quickly transpose. We'll continue by fianchettoing. White now has two options...} 5...Bb7 6.O-O {White's other option is to castle. This is half as common as Nbd2. Against O-O, I also suggest ...Bd6. The idea is, once again, that we are looking to trade off our dark-squared bishop, which wouldn't have great prospects on e7, for the f4-bishop. White now has some options...} 6...Bd6 7.Ne5 {White might decline the trade w/Ne5. This is seen about 23% of the time and it runs into a really savage trap! We'll push ...g5 & attack the bishop! White now has two options...} 7...g5 8.Bg3 {Seeing that Bxg5 runs into a world of trouble after ...Rg8, White goes Bg3. We'll still go after White w/...h5!} 8...h5 9.Nf3 {Nf3 is a more resilient response. Against this, we'll trade the bishops.} 9...Bxg3 10.fxg3 {White should recapture w/the f-pawn. We'll proceed by pushing ...h4!} 10...h4 11.gxh4 {Best is for White to take the h-pawn.} 11...gxh4 {And we'll recapture with the g-pawn. Even with perfect defense, Black is doing very well. The b7-bishop is a beast, the queen is joining the party via Qe7-Qg7 & ...h3 is on the cards.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays The London / Study 2: All London Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/0942d4f6-426e-49a6-a125-86478279dc5f/studies/c96b4238-a618-4010-8894-2ff4f5f1cabe/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study! Instead of playing Bf4 on the second move, White might opt to start w/Nf3. This study will dive deeper into the typical London System setup which involves Nf3 + Bf4 + e3 and Bd3. Let's take a look!} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {White develops the king's knight. This move is almost as common as the move 2. Bf4 seen in study one. We'll develop our king's knight to f6 and take more control over the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Bf4 {White develops the c1-bishop to f4. This is the signature square for the dark-squared bishop in the London. As per usual, we'll proceed with ...e6. This move strengthens the center and opens up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.e3 {White almost always proceeds w/e3. This move adds support to d4 and opens up the light-squared bishop. And we'll proceed with ...b6, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop.} 4...b6 5.Bd3 {White develops the f1-bishop to d3. Other moves, such as Nbd2 or c4, are possible, but they quickly transpose. We'll continue by fianchettoing. White now has two options...} 5...Bb7 6.Nbd2 {Most often, White goes for Nbd2. This is a flexible move - White doesn't commit their king and waits for Black. We'll offer a bishop trade w/...Bd6! We're looking to trade off our dark-squared bishop, which wouldn't have great prospects on e7, for White's active bishop on f4. If White is threatening e4, we should capture the dark squared bishop.} 6...Bd6 7.Ne5 {White can decline the trade w/Ne5. This is seen about 18% of the time. I suggest we proceed with ...Qe7. This innocuous looking move actually comes with a threat: If it were Black's move, Black would take the g2-pawn and g7 wouldn't hang after Rg1 Bb7.} 7...Qe7 8.O-O {White proceeds by castling their king. We'll go for ...Rg8, preparing ...g5 and signaling our aggressive intentions!} 8...Rg8 9.Ndf3 {White jumps the d2-knight into f3. This prevents the ...g5 advance.} 9...Nc6 {We'll develop our final minor piece. Our plan is now to castle queenside and launch a kingside pawn storm! A very complex & dynamic position. White needs to defend accurately in order not to get blown off the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 1: Lines After 2.Nf3 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/52aa556e-5007-4e6f-aeed-5bca502a5174/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter of our side lines, we'll take a look at some of White's peculiar approaches on second move that abstain from the usual 2.c4 principle. Let's take a look at 2.Nf3 setups in Study 1.} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {Instead of placing two pawns in the center, White activates the knight immediately. This move is played 16% of the time in the Lichess Masters database. The reason is that White probably doesn't want to commit to anything early and leaves their options open. We'll continue with our usual setup and activate the knight to f6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {This is the only position of independent value after d4 and Nf3 by White. 3. c4 or 3. g3 are covered everywhere else, and so is 3. Bf4. We'll keep developing with ...e6 and build a strong pawn chain in the center.} 3...e6 4.Nbd2 {4.Nbd2 is actually by far the most popular move among chess masters, played 83% of the time. However, it's not as natural as the other moves, so you may not see it as often. The idea is to enable prospects of e4 in the future, while also leaving room for the c-pawn to move if needed. We'll just keep developing the kingside and unpin our knight on f6.} 4...Be7 5.e3 {Another possibility is White going for e3 instead of the more aggressive e4 approach. This move is usually supported by c3 or c4 in the future. We'll start activating the kingside. Getting another piece to control the e4-square before we castle would be great.} 5...b6 6.Bd3 {White will keep developing... And we'll go for the fianchetto.} 6...Bb7 7.O-O {Once they castle... It's time for us to get out of the way as well. Now White has two main options.} 7...O-O 8.c3 {The more solid approach by White is going for c3. Once we activate our pieces, do you remember what the usual "first move" in the middlegame is? It's taking advantage of that e4-square control and placing a knight there! Now there's tension between the bishops.} 8...Ne4 9.Bxe7 {White will most likely trade. And we'll recapture, of course.} 9...Qxe7 10.Qc2 {If White activates the queen, the threat of capturing the knight in the center is reinitiated. Let's hang on to our strong piece. Although we block the bishop with this move, maintaining a "thorn" in White's position is more than sufficient to compensate for that.} 10...d5 11.Ne5 {White will probably use the hole in the e5-square and jump there with the knight. We can't attack it with ...d6, but that's ok. We have other plans.} 11...c5 {We'll strike in the center with ...c5 and challenge White's center. The main ideas from now on are to activate the knight to c6 and challenge White's piece on e5. Then, we;ll often activate the rook to c8 and play on the c-file if it opens up.} *

