[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/52aa556e-5007-4e6f-aeed-5bca502a5174/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter of our side lines, we'll take a look at some of White's peculiar approaches on second move that abstain from the usual 2.c4 principle. Let's take a look at 2.Nf3 setups in Study 1.} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {Instead of placing two pawns in the center, White activates the knight immediately. This move is played 16% of the time in the Lichess Masters database. The reason is that White probably doesn't want to commit to anything early and leaves their options open. We'll continue with our usual setup and activate the knight to f6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {This is the only position of independent value after d4 and Nf3 by White. 3. c4 or 3. g3 are covered everywhere else, and so is 3. Bf4. We'll keep developing with ...e6 and build a strong pawn chain in the center.} 3...e6 4.Nbd2 {4.Nbd2 is actually by far the most popular move among chess masters, played 83% of the time. However, it's not as natural as the other moves, so you may not see it as often. The idea is to enable prospects of e4 in the future, while also leaving room for the c-pawn to move if needed. We'll just keep developing the kingside and unpin our knight on f6.} 4...Be7 5.e3 {Another possibility is White going for e3 instead of the more aggressive e4 approach. This move is usually supported by c3 or c4 in the future. We'll start activating the kingside. Getting another piece to control the e4-square before we castle would be great.} 5...b6 6.Bd3 {White will keep developing... And we'll go for the fianchetto.} 6...Bb7 7.O-O {Once they castle... It's time for us to get out of the way as well. Now White has two main options.} 7...O-O 8.c3 {The more solid approach by White is going for c3. Once we activate our pieces, do you remember what the usual "first move" in the middlegame is? It's taking advantage of that e4-square control and placing a knight there! Now there's tension between the bishops.} 8...Ne4 9.Bxe7 {White will most likely trade. And we'll recapture, of course.} 9...Qxe7 10.Ne5 {If White tries to get greedy and grab space now...} 10...d6 {They will run right into ...d6, attacking the knight right away.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 1: Lines After 2.Nf3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/52aa556e-5007-4e6f-aeed-5bca502a5174/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter of our side lines, we'll take a look at some of White's peculiar approaches on second move that abstain from the usual 2.c4 principle. Let's take a look at 2.Nf3 setups in Study 1.} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {Instead of placing two pawns in the center, White activates the knight immediately. This move is played 16% of the time in the Lichess Masters database. The reason is that White probably doesn't want to commit to anything early and leaves their options open. We'll continue with our usual setup and activate the knight to f6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {This is the only position of independent value after d4 and Nf3 by White. 3. c4 or 3. g3 are covered everywhere else, and so is 3. Bf4. We'll keep developing with ...e6 and build a strong pawn chain in the center.} 3...e6 4.e3 {Although not the most popular move amongst masters, I put e3 as the main line because it's the most natural line for development you should remember. First, we'll unpin the knight.} 4...Be7 5.Bd3 {White will continue activating the bishop. The d3-square is most active. We'll continue developing in a standard manner as well.} 5...b6 6.O-O {Once White castles... We'll get another piece into the game to control the e4-square.} 6...Bb7 7.c4 {The most active approach is placing two pawns in the center. This doesn't bother us, though. Let's castle out of the way.} 7...O-O 8.Nc3 {White activates the knight to c3... And here's an important idea. The way to activate the queen is via the light squares here. We would like to place the queen to g6, from which it controls e4, so that we can play ...Ne4.} 8...Qe8 9.Re1 {Continuing in a traditional manner is Re1. White might be preparing e4. But we won't let them.} 9...Ne4 10.Bxe7 {Most of the time, the bishops get traded on e7. Let's recapture.} 10...Qxe7 11.Bxe4 {If White really wants to simplify the game and trade on e4 as well, that's not a problem. We'll simply recapture with the pawn and attack the knight on f3.} 11...fxe4 12.Nd2 {The knight will probably maneuver back to d2... And we'll solidify our structure with ...d5. I'd say this line is very logical in terms of how the moves basically play themselves.} 12...d5 13.cxd5 {If White takes... We'll recapture, still defending the center. They've only improved our structure.} 13...exd5 14.Qb3 {The idea of capturing on d5 is to play Qb3, though, with pressure on the d5-pawn. We don't really want to play ...c6 and block our bishop... But we'll play the amazing ...Qf7, defending the pawn. And, what's more important, the queen and the rook together attack the pawn on f2! White's Re1-idea hasn't paid off.} 14...Qf7 15.Rf1 {They go back.} 15...c6 {Now it's fine to play ...c6, as we have a concrete plan on how to get the bishop out. It's going to be ...Ba6, attacking the rook on f1. White definitely needs to be careful as to how they continue!} *

