1.d4 {In this chapter of our side lines, we'll take a look at some of White's peculiar approaches on second move that abstain from the usual 2.c4 principle. Let's take a look at 2.Nf3 setups in Study 1.} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {Instead of placing two pawns in the center, White activates the knight immediately. This move is played 16% of the time in the Lichess Masters database. The reason is that White probably doesn't want to commit to anything early and leaves their options open. We'll continue with our usual setup and activate the knight to f6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {This is the only position of independent value after d4 and Nf3 by White. 3. c4 or 3. g3 are covered everywhere else, and so is 3. Bf4. We'll keep developing with ...e6 and build a strong pawn chain in the center.} 3...e6 4.Nbd2 {4.Nbd2 is actually by far the most popular move among chess masters, played 83% of the time. However, it's not as natural as the other moves, so you may not see it as often. The idea is to enable prospects of e4 in the future, while also leaving room for the c-pawn to move if needed. We'll just keep developing the kingside and unpin our knight on f6.} 4...Be7 5.e3 {Another possibility is White going for e3 instead of the more aggressive e4 approach. This move is usually supported by c3 or c4 in the future. We'll start activating the kingside. Getting another piece to control the e4-square before we castle would be great.} 5...b6 6.Bd3 {White will keep developing... And we'll go for the fianchetto.} 6...Bb7 7.O-O {Once they castle... It's time for us to get out of the way as well. Now White has two main options.} 7...O-O 8.c4 {Going for c4 definitely grabs more space. But it shouldn't change our plans.} 8...Ne4 {We'll get the knight into the center, supported by the bishop and the pawn. In the near future, a trade of bishops will definitely occur, but we'll have a stable position because of our control of the e4-square.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 1: Lines After 2.Nf3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/52aa556e-5007-4e6f-aeed-5bca502a5174/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter of our side lines, we'll take a look at some of White's peculiar approaches on second move that abstain from the usual 2.c4 principle. Let's take a look at 2.Nf3 setups in Study 1.} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {Instead of placing two pawns in the center, White activates the knight immediately. This move is played 16% of the time in the Lichess Masters database. The reason is that White probably doesn't want to commit to anything early and leaves their options open. We'll continue with our usual setup and activate the knight to f6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {This is the only position of independent value after d4 and Nf3 by White. 3. c4 or 3. g3 are covered everywhere else, and so is 3. Bf4. We'll keep developing with ...e6 and build a strong pawn chain in the center.} 3...e6 4.Nbd2 {4.Nbd2 is actually by far the most popular move among chess masters, played 83% of the time. However, it's not as natural as the other moves, so you may not see it as often. The idea is to enable prospects of e4 in the future, while also leaving room for the c-pawn to move if needed. We'll just keep developing the kingside and unpin our knight on f6.} 4...Be7 5.Bxf6 {The most popular move is to capture the knight. That's why White played Bg5 and Nbd2; they want to remove the defender of the e4-square and play e4! We'll recapture with the bishop.} 5...Bxf6 6.e4 {Now White places two pawns in the center. Capturing on e4 would only activate their knight and prove their point. Instead, let's put more pressure on the e4-pawn and force White to capture one of the pawns!} 6...d5 7.exf5 {Capturing on f5 or on d5 makes no difference, we'll recapture with the e-pawn either way and leave ourselves controlling the e4-square again. The most logical continuation.} 7...exf5 8.Qe2+ {If White tries to be swift and give a check on e2 before we can get to do it... We'll still block with the queen.} 8...Qe7 9.Qxe7+ {The difference is, when White captures...} 9...Kxe7 {We'll recapture with the king! We don't mind losing castling rights here, as we'll get our bishop to e6 and centralize our h-rook soon. An equal position so far.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 1: Lines After 2.Nf3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/52aa556e-5007-4e6f-aeed-5bca502a5174/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter of our side lines, we'll take a look at some of White's peculiar approaches on second move that abstain from the usual 2.c4 principle. Let's take a look at 2.Nf3 setups in Study 1.} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {Instead of placing two pawns in the center, White activates the knight immediately. This move is played 16% of the time in the Lichess Masters database. The reason is that White probably doesn't want to commit to anything early and leaves their options open. We'll continue with our usual setup and activate the knight to f6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {This is the only position of independent value after d4 and Nf3 by White. 3. c4 or 3. g3 are covered everywhere else, and so is 3. Bf4. We'll keep developing with ...e6 and build a strong pawn chain in the center.} 3...e6 4.Nbd2 {4.Nbd2 is actually by far the most popular move among chess masters, played 83% of the time. However, it's not as natural as the other moves, so you may not see it as often. The idea is to enable prospects of e4 in the future, while also leaving room for the c-pawn to move if needed. We'll just keep developing the kingside and unpin our knight on f6.} 4...Be7 5.Bxf6 {The most popular move is to capture the knight. That's why White played Bg5 and Nbd2; they want to remove the defender of the e4-square and play e4! We'll recapture with the bishop.} 5...Bxf6 6.e4 {Now White places two pawns in the center. Capturing on e4 would only activate their knight and prove their point. Instead, let's put more pressure on the e4-pawn and force White to capture one of the pawns!} 6...d5 7.exf5 {Capturing on f5 or on d5 makes no difference, we'll recapture with the e-pawn either way and leave ourselves controlling the e4-square again. The most logical continuation.} 7...exf5 8.Bd3 {Now White's pieces are a little bit cramped with the knight on d2. However, that shouldn't stop them from developing the rest of the kingside. I recommend taking advantage of the open e-file and giving a check before White castles. Trading queens doesn't bother us at all, as you'll see.} 8...Qe7+ 9.Qe2 {White will probably block with the queen, as they have just developed the bishop to d3. We'll capture. The idea is that White either has to capture with the king and lose castling rights, or capture with the bishop and lose a tempo!} 9...Qxe2+ 10.Bxe2 {Capturing with the bishop is more sensible.} 10...O-O {Now we'll castle, with an equal position. A lot of the times White will castle queenside here and try to quickly centralize the rooks. Our idea should be to activate the knight to d7, and often reroute it to e4 via f6. Our dark-squared bishop could retreat to a square like d8 and c7.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 1: Lines After 2.Nf3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/52aa556e-5007-4e6f-aeed-5bca502a5174/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter of our side lines, we'll take a look at some of White's peculiar approaches on second move that abstain from the usual 2.c4 principle. Let's take a look at 2.Nf3 setups in Study 1.} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {Instead of placing two pawns in the center, White activates the knight immediately. This move is played 16% of the time in the Lichess Masters database. The reason is that White probably doesn't want to commit to anything early and leaves their options open. We'll continue with our usual setup and activate the knight to f6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {This is the only position of independent value after d4 and Nf3 by White. 3. c4 or 3. g3 are covered everywhere else, and so is 3. Bf4. We'll keep developing with ...e6 and build a strong pawn chain in the center.} 3...e6 4.Nc3 {White going for Nc3 is similar to chapter we've seen before. We'll immediately pin the knight, with the intention to capture.} 4...Bb4 5.e3 {Not going for a3 is smarter by White, as we'll probably capture anyway. This way they don't lose a tempo. We'll take the opportunity and castle.} 5...O-O 6.Bd3 {Normal development by White. We'll continue developing as well.} 6...b6 7.O-O {Once White castles, their knight isn't pinned anymore. So we'll capture now.} 7...Bxc3 8.bxc3 {And double their pawns.} 8...Bb7 {This line should come the most natural to you, with standard development. We'll jump the knight into the center and probably activate the queen on the light squares and unpin the knight on f6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 1: Lines After 2.Nf3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/52aa556e-5007-4e6f-aeed-5bca502a5174/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter of our side lines, we'll take a look at some of White's peculiar approaches on second move that abstain from the usual 2.c4 principle. Let's take a look at 2.Nf3 setups in Study 1.} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {Instead of placing two pawns in the center, White activates the knight immediately. This move is played 16% of the time in the Lichess Masters database. The reason is that White probably doesn't want to commit to anything early and leaves their options open. We'll continue with our usual setup and activate the knight to f6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {This is the only position of independent value after d4 and Nf3 by White. 3. c4 or 3. g3 are covered everywhere else, and so is 3. Bf4. We'll keep developing with ...e6 and build a strong pawn chain in the center.} 3...e6 4.Nbd2 {4.Nbd2 is actually by far the most popular move among chess masters, played 83% of the time. However, it's not as natural as the other moves, so you may not see it as often. The idea is to enable prospects of e4 in the future, while also leaving room for the c-pawn to move if needed. We'll just keep developing the kingside and unpin our knight on f6.} 4...Be7 5.Bxf6 {The most popular move is to capture the knight. That's why White played Bg5 and Nbd2; they want to remove the defender of the e4-square and play e4! We'll recapture with the bishop.} 5...Bxf6 6.e4 {Now White places two pawns in the center. Capturing on e4 would only activate their knight and prove their point. Instead, let's put more pressure on the e4-pawn and force White to capture one of the pawns!} 6...d5 7.e5 {A tricky move is avoiding the trades in the center and shoving the e-pawn forward here. Our bishop is under attack. Let's bring it back to e7. Now our main idea becomes chipping away at their extended structure with ...c5, and White can't jump to e5 with their knight anymore.} 7...Be7 8.Bd3 {When they develop... First, we'll get out of the way.} 8...O-O 9.c3 {Going for c3 strengthens White's dark-squared structure. But we'll undermine it right away. If White captures, their pawns will be split. If not, we'll just keep developing on the light squares.} 9...c5 10.O-O {White castles.} 10...b6 {Now we'll open up the light-squared bishop and look to activate it to a6 to possibly trade. Our knight can go to c6 and keep pressuring White's center. Our position feels a little bit cramped but if we find the right setup to play against White's structure, we'll be completely fine.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 1: Lines After 2.Nf3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/52aa556e-5007-4e6f-aeed-5bca502a5174/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter of our side lines, we'll take a look at some of White's peculiar approaches on second move that abstain from the usual 2.c4 principle. Let's take a look at 2.Nf3 setups in Study 1.} 1...f5 2.Nf3 {Instead of placing two pawns in the center, White activates the knight immediately. This move is played 16% of the time in the Lichess Masters database. The reason is that White probably doesn't want to commit to anything early and leaves their options open. We'll continue with our usual setup and activate the knight to f6.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {This is the only position of independent value after d4 and Nf3 by White. 3. c4 or 3. g3 are covered everywhere else, and so is 3. Bf4. We'll keep developing with ...e6 and build a strong pawn chain in the center.} 3...e6 4.Nc3 {White going for Nc3 is similar to chapter we've seen before. We'll immediately pin the knight, with the intention to capture.} 4...Bb4 5.a3 {If White goes for a3 immediately, that just goes according to our plan. Let's take.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {Their pawns will be doubled. And we'll ask the bishop on g5 a question. Can it go to h4?} 6...h6 7.Bxf6 {If White decides to capture...} 7...Qxf6 {We'll just take back with the queen. From here on, you know the plans. We'll castle, play ...d6 to control the center, activate the knight to d7 and the bishop to b7, probably.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 2: The Tricky 2.Nc3 and 2.Bg5 Setups / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/3938883d-56f6-4a5a-a1da-d3a6daa5a3e1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of our side lines, we'll take a look at White playing Nc3 on move 2 instead of c4, trying to go for an early e4 or g4. Another possibility is the immediate Bg5, looking to capture our knight on f6 when it arrives there. We need to be prepared for both!} 1...f5 2.Bg5 {Definitely an aggressive approach is White going for Bg5 immediately. The idea is to remove our knight from the f6-square and try to disrupt our structure. We'll let them do it.} 2...Nf6 3.Bxf6 {White takes. Let's recapture with the e-pawn and open up our bishop.} 3...exf6 4.e3 {A more peaceful approach is to go for e3, strengthening the central structure and opening up the bishop. Let's play in the center with ...d5.} 4...d5 5.c4 {We also have to be ready for c4 now, as it can come at any moment. As in the 5.Bd3 line, we'll defend the pawn with ...Be6.} 5...Be6 6.Qb3 {Another way of continuing the attack on the d5-pawn is Qb3. Our b7-pawn is also under attack. Let's set a trap for White!} 6...Nc6 7.Qxb7 {If White captures, they're already lost without even knowing it. We'll play the amazing ...Nb4! We place the knight on the b4-square and look to fork White's king and rook. This move blocks the queen's retreating path, which will be extremely useful in the near future, as you'll see.} 7...Nb4 8.Na3 {The most logical way of defending against ...Nc2 is Na3. If you remember this move, you'll realize that White's queen is suddenly trapped on b7! All the squares around it are controlled by our pieces.} 8...a6 9.c5 {White might try to free up the bishop on f1, but it doesn't help. We'll attack the queen.} 9...Rb8 10.Qa7 {The only square it can move to is a7. Once it does...} 10...Qc8 {We'll control all the squares the queen can escape to! Next, we'll play ...Ra8 back. White won't be able to save the queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 2: The Tricky 2.Nc3 and 2.Bg5 Setups / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/3938883d-56f6-4a5a-a1da-d3a6daa5a3e1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of our side lines, we'll take a look at White playing Nc3 on move 2 instead of c4, trying to go for an early e4 or g4. Another possibility is the immediate Bg5, looking to capture our knight on f6 when it arrives there. We need to be prepared for both!} 1...f5 2.Bg5 {Definitely an aggressive approach is White going for Bg5 immediately. The idea is to remove our knight from the f6-square and try to disrupt our structure. We'll let them do it.} 2...Nf6 3.Bxf6 {White takes. Let's recapture with the e-pawn and open up our bishop.} 3...exf6 4.c4 {If White tries the usual approach with placing two pawns in the center, it's important to realize there's not a knight on c3 yet. So we can go for the immediate d5!} 4...d5 5.cxd5 {The most challenging line is White capturing in the center. We are forced to recapture with the queen and get it to the center.} 5...Qxd5 6.Nc3 {It will probably get attacked immediately... But we'll pin the knight while developing the bishop. White can't capture the queen, of course.} 6...Bb4 7.e3 {They might go for e3, trying to open up the light-squared bishop. But we'll just keep developing. I like ...Nc6 here, not blocking the bishop with ...Nd7 and maintaining some pressure in the center.} 7...Nc6 8.Nf3 {If White develops with Nf3, there's a great move I want you to remember.} 8...f4 {We'll go for the sneaky ...f4! This move will completely disrupt White's structure around the king and the center. If White captures, their d4-pawn becomes weak. Next, we'll look to activate the bishop to g4 and then we can choose which way to castle. Often, it will be the queenside!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 2: The Tricky 2.Nc3 and 2.Bg5 Setups / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/3938883d-56f6-4a5a-a1da-d3a6daa5a3e1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of our side lines, we'll take a look at White playing Nc3 on move 2 instead of c4, trying to go for an early e4 or g4. Another possibility is the immediate Bg5, looking to capture our knight on f6 when it arrives there. We need to be prepared for both!} 1...f5 2.Nc3 {Another move White could play is Nc3 here. Although blocking the c-pawn, the idea is to try and achieve an early e4 or g4, as you'll see. We'll continue in our usual style.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {Our knight controls the e4-square. So, White often wants to get rid of it! Let's open up the bishop. Although we have to play this move in order to develop, it gives White the chance to push e4 immediately.} 3...e6 4.Bxf6 {White could also capture here and then go for e4. We'll recapture with the queen.} 4...Qxf6 5.e4 {Once e4 comes... We'll do the same thing White did to us. We'll restrict the piece that controls the e4-square!} 5...Bb4 6.exf5 {If White chooses to capture on f5, we don't need to recapture right away. We can actually castle out of the way here. White can't keep taking because of the weak f2-pawn.} 6...O-O 7.Nf3 {They will probably activate the knight and reintroduce the threat of capturing on e6. We'll finish the job we started on the queenside...} 7...Bxc3+ 8.bxc3 {And once White recaptures, we'll finally take back. Now the e-file is open, but so is the g8-a2 diagonal, on which our king stands.} 8...exf5 9.Bc4+ {White often uses that to their advantage to develop a piece with tempo. Let's get out of the way.} 9...Kh8 10.O-O {White will castle and disallow any checks on the e-file... And we'll keep developing. The most natural is ...Nc6, with ideas of ...d6 to open up our bishop.} 10...Nc6 11.Re1 {White can occupy the e-file, but that's fine. The rook is not dangerous. Let's continue developing and defend against Ne5.} 11...d6 12.Qd2 {One of the ideas White might try to go for is Qd2-Qg5. This would basically force a queen trade and leave White with more active pieces and center control.} 12...h6 {So, we'll stop that. Our plan is going to be simple; activate the bishop to b7, and get a rook to contest the e-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 2: The Tricky 2.Nc3 and 2.Bg5 Setups / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/3938883d-56f6-4a5a-a1da-d3a6daa5a3e1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of our side lines, we'll take a look at White playing Nc3 on move 2 instead of c4, trying to go for an early e4 or g4. Another possibility is the immediate Bg5, looking to capture our knight on f6 when it arrives there. We need to be prepared for both!} 1...f5 2.Nc3 {Another move White could play is Nc3 here. Although blocking the c-pawn, the idea is to try and achieve an early e4 or g4, as you'll see. We'll continue in our usual style.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {Our knight controls the e4-square. So, White often wants to get rid of it! Let's open up the bishop. Although we have to play this move in order to develop, it gives White the chance to push e4 immediately.} 3...e6 4.e4 {By far the most popular move, played about 90% of the time, is e4. That's what White was going for anyway; central control. Let's not give it to them.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe4 {If the knight recaptures and puts pressure on our pinned knight, we aren't scared. We've always got the bishop to unpin and defend. Now the threat of ...Nxe4 is introduced.} 5...Be7 6.Bxf6 {So, White will probably capture, leaving their knight in the center. After we take back, White has multiple options.} 6...Bxf6 7.Nf3 {The most traditional one being Nf3, simply developing on the kingside. I recommend retreating here and preserving the bishop pair.} 7...Be7 8.Bd3 {If White isn't that aggressive and simply continues developing, that's fine. They won't get in any trouble, but so won't we. Let's grab space in the center with tempo.} 8...d5 9.Ng3 {White retreats to g3, which only allows us to keep expanding. Striking in the center and challenging White's. Capturing here only activates our bishop...} 9...c5 10.c3 {So White will probably choose to defend the d4-pawn. We'll keep going at it.} 10...Nc6 11.O-O {The pawn is sufficiently defended for now, so White castles. Before we do anything else, I suggest capturing here. This allows us to place a rook on the c-file later if we want to.} 11...cxd4 12.cxd4 {White recaptures. And let's castle. We should only castle once White has castled.} 12...O-O 13.Re1 {White occupies the e-file and attacks our pawn...} 13...Bd6 {And we'll improve the position of our bishop, with a balanced game. I'd suggest developing the pieces towards the kingside/center here.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 2: The Tricky 2.Nc3 and 2.Bg5 Setups / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/3938883d-56f6-4a5a-a1da-d3a6daa5a3e1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of our side lines, we'll take a look at White playing Nc3 on move 2 instead of c4, trying to go for an early e4 or g4. Another possibility is the immediate Bg5, looking to capture our knight on f6 when it arrives there. We need to be prepared for both!} 1...f5 2.Nc3 {Another move White could play is Nc3 here. Although blocking the c-pawn, the idea is to try and achieve an early e4 or g4, as you'll see. We'll continue in our usual style.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {Our knight controls the e4-square. So, White often wants to get rid of it! Let's open up the bishop. Although we have to play this move in order to develop, it gives White the chance to push e4 immediately.} 3...e6 4.e4 {By far the most popular move, played about 90% of the time, is e4. That's what White was going for anyway; central control. Let's not give it to them.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe4 {If the knight recaptures and puts pressure on our pinned knight, we aren't scared. We've always got the bishop to unpin and defend. Now the threat of ...Nxe4 is introduced.} 5...Be7 6.Bxf6 {So, White will probably capture, leaving their knight in the center. After we take back, White has multiple options.} 6...Bxf6 7.Nf3 {The most traditional one being Nf3, simply developing on the kingside. I recommend retreating here and preserving the bishop pair.} 7...Be7 8.Ne5 {If White really wants to play aggressively and threaten Qh5... Let them come!} 8...O-O 9.Qh5 {The queen lands on the active h5-square and eyes our king. But that's fine, as there aren't any threats at the moment. And there won't be, as far as we are concerned! We'll kick out the knight first.} 9...d6 10.Ng4 {Once it retreats, probably to g4... I suggest we simply keep developing and pressure the center. Our rook and bishop together nicely control the f6-square.} 10...Nc6 11.c3 {White will defend with c3. Let's kick out the other knight as well! Slowly but surely, we are forcing White to back down, while expanding at the same time. It seems like their attack isn't really working!} 11...d5 12.Ng3 {Once the knight retreats...} 12...g6 {Finally, we'll attack the queen. Since White has chosen to place the knights on g4 and g3, there aren't really many squares for the queen apart from h3. White will be very passive, and we'll continue going at them with ...e5. Black is winning here!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 2: The Tricky 2.Nc3 and 2.Bg5 Setups / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/3938883d-56f6-4a5a-a1da-d3a6daa5a3e1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of our side lines, we'll take a look at White playing Nc3 on move 2 instead of c4, trying to go for an early e4 or g4. Another possibility is the immediate Bg5, looking to capture our knight on f6 when it arrives there. We need to be prepared for both!} 1...f5 2.Bg5 {Definitely an aggressive approach is White going for Bg5 immediately. The idea is to remove our knight from the f6-square and try to disrupt our structure. We'll let them do it.} 2...Nf6 3.Bxf6 {White takes. Let's recapture with the e-pawn and open up our bishop.} 3...exf6 4.e3 {A more peaceful approach is to go for e3, strengthening the central structure and opening up the bishop. Let's play in the center with ...d5.} 4...d5 5.Bd3 {White developing the bishop to d3 is not an issue. We can just continue activating our pieces. I like ...Be6 here, as it keeps vision on both the f5- and the d5-pawns.} 5...Be6 6.Qf3 {White might continue the pressure with Qf3. Going for ...g6 might be too weakening for our structure, but here's another idea behind ...Be6. We'll play ...Qd7 and defend the f5-pawn.} 6...Qd7 7.Ne2 {White might continue developing with Ne2, trying to go for Ng3 and add another attacker to the pawn. And we'll go for ...Nc6, aiming to castle long!} 7...Nc6 8.Ng3 {If White plays Ng3, we are ready.} 8...g6 {Now we can play ...g6 and castle queenside soon.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 2: The Tricky 2.Nc3 and 2.Bg5 Setups / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/3938883d-56f6-4a5a-a1da-d3a6daa5a3e1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of our side lines, we'll take a look at White playing Nc3 on move 2 instead of c4, trying to go for an early e4 or g4. Another possibility is the immediate Bg5, looking to capture our knight on f6 when it arrives there. We need to be prepared for both!} 1...f5 2.Bg5 {Definitely an aggressive approach is White going for Bg5 immediately. The idea is to remove our knight from the f6-square and try to disrupt our structure. We'll let them do it.} 2...Nf6 3.Bxf6 {White takes. Let's recapture with the e-pawn and open up our bishop.} 3...exf6 4.e3 {A more peaceful approach is to go for e3, strengthening the central structure and opening up the bishop. Let's play in the center with ...d5.} 4...d5 5.c4 {We also have to be ready for c4 now, as it can come at any moment. As in the 5.Bd3 line, we'll defend the pawn with ...Be6.} 5...Be6 6.cxd5 {If White captures in the center... We'll recapture with the bishop.} 6...Bxd5 7.Nc3 {Nc3 attacks it, but we already know how to respond to that. Let's pin the knight.} 7...Bb4 8.Ne2 {If White wants to preserve the pressure on the center, they will have to support the knight with Ne2. Let's castle out of the way.} 8...O-O 9.a3 {If White attacks our bishop, there is no point in retreating anymore and letting White get the momentum. Let's capture.} 9...Bxc3+ 10.Nxc3 {White will recapture with the knight, attacking the bishop again. But we have that one move that changes their plans a little bit. Let's challenge the e3-pawn! In this instance, there is no pressure on d4, so White could even capture.} 10...f4 11.Nxd5 {If White decides to capture in the center... We'll just take. There is one thing we need to be careful about, however.} 11...Qxd5 12.Rc1 {White might play Rc1 and try to go for Bc4! Always be on the lookout for tactics on the f8-a2 diagonal when you move your f-pawn as Black.} 12...Kh8 {Simply sliding the king out of the way saves our queen. Our plan is, if allowed, to capture on e3 and then develop normally with ...Nc6 and get the rooks to the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 2: The Tricky 2.Nc3 and 2.Bg5 Setups / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/3938883d-56f6-4a5a-a1da-d3a6daa5a3e1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of our side lines, we'll take a look at White playing Nc3 on move 2 instead of c4, trying to go for an early e4 or g4. Another possibility is the immediate Bg5, looking to capture our knight on f6 when it arrives there. We need to be prepared for both!} 1...f5 2.Bg5 {Definitely an aggressive approach is White going for Bg5 immediately. The idea is to remove our knight from the f6-square and try to disrupt our structure. We'll let them do it.} 2...Nf6 3.Bxf6 {White takes. Let's recapture with the e-pawn and open up our bishop.} 3...exf6 4.e3 {A more peaceful approach is to go for e3, strengthening the central structure and opening up the bishop. Let's play in the center with ...d5.} 4...d5 5.c4 {We also have to be ready for c4 now, as it can come at any moment. As in the 5.Bd3 line, we'll defend the pawn with ...Be6.} 5...Be6 6.cxd5 {If White captures in the center... We'll recapture with the bishop.} 6...Bxd5 7.Nc3 {Nc3 attacks it, but we already know how to respond to that. Let's pin the knight.} 7...Bb4 8.Ne2 {If White wants to preserve the pressure on the center, they will have to support the knight with Ne2. Let's castle out of the way.} 8...O-O 9.a3 {If White attacks our bishop, there is no point in retreating anymore and letting White get the momentum. Let's capture.} 9...Bxc3+ 10.Nxc3 {White will recapture with the knight, attacking the bishop again. But we have that one move that changes their plans a little bit. Let's challenge the e3-pawn! In this instance, there is no pressure on d4, so White could even capture.} 10...f4 11.exf4 {If they do...} 11...Re8+ {The e-file suddenly opens up and we can deliver a check on it. White will probably have to block with the bishop, which is in itself uncomfortable, as the g2-pawn would hang. In the future, I like playing ...c6 to control the d5-square if White wants to push d5 and activating the knight on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 2: The Tricky 2.Nc3 and 2.Bg5 Setups / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/3938883d-56f6-4a5a-a1da-d3a6daa5a3e1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of our side lines, we'll take a look at White playing Nc3 on move 2 instead of c4, trying to go for an early e4 or g4. Another possibility is the immediate Bg5, looking to capture our knight on f6 when it arrives there. We need to be prepared for both!} 1...f5 2.Nc3 {Another move White could play is Nc3 here. Although blocking the c-pawn, the idea is to try and achieve an early e4 or g4, as you'll see. We'll continue in our usual style.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {Our knight controls the e4-square. So, White often wants to get rid of it! Let's open up the bishop. Although we have to play this move in order to develop, it gives White the chance to push e4 immediately.} 3...e6 4.Bxf6 {White could also capture here and then go for e4. We'll recapture with the queen.} 4...Qxf6 5.e4 {Once e4 comes... We'll do the same thing White did to us. We'll restrict the piece that controls the e4-square!} 5...Bb4 6.e5 {A more direct approach by White is shoving the e-pawn forward and attacking our queen. I recommend taking here with check and doubling White's c-pawns.} 6...Bxc3+ 7.bxc3 {They will take back... And let's retreat to e7. From this square, our queen is still active but safe.} 7...Qe7 8.Nf3 {A natural response by White, and we'll respond naturally as well. Let's open up the b7-square for our bishop.} 8...b6 9.Bd3 {White continues developing... And so will we.} 9...Bb7 10.O-O {Both sides will castle.} 10...O-O {White does have some space, but we have got a pretty comfortable position. A good idea would be to chip away at White's structure with ...c5, activate the knight to c6 and get the a-rook into the game.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 2: The Tricky 2.Nc3 and 2.Bg5 Setups / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/3938883d-56f6-4a5a-a1da-d3a6daa5a3e1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of our side lines, we'll take a look at White playing Nc3 on move 2 instead of c4, trying to go for an early e4 or g4. Another possibility is the immediate Bg5, looking to capture our knight on f6 when it arrives there. We need to be prepared for both!} 1...f5 2.Nc3 {Another move White could play is Nc3 here. Although blocking the c-pawn, the idea is to try and achieve an early e4 or g4, as you'll see. We'll continue in our usual style.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {Our knight controls the e4-square. So, White often wants to get rid of it! Let's open up the bishop. Although we have to play this move in order to develop, it gives White the chance to push e4 immediately.} 3...e6 4.e4 {By far the most popular move, played about 90% of the time, is e4. That's what White was going for anyway; central control. Let's not give it to them.} 4...fxe4 5.Nxe4 {If the knight recaptures and puts pressure on our pinned knight, we aren't scared. We've always got the bishop to unpin and defend. Now the threat of ...Nxe4 is introduced.} 5...Be7 6.Bxf6 {So, White will probably capture, leaving their knight in the center. After we take back, White has multiple options.} 6...Bxf6 7.Qh5+ {Another aggressive approach by White is giving a check here immediately and provoking ...g6. We have to block.} 7...g6 8.Qh6 {White's idea is to move the queen to h6 and prevent us from castling. This looks uncomfortable, but is completely fine. Just remember to control the g7-square and look to retreat the bishop to g7. Which we'll start doing} 8...Qe7 9.O-O-O {If White doesn't capture our bishop and castles, our plan for the future is easy. We'll kick out the queen and then finally get some pawns to the center so that White can feel our presence! First, their queen has to move.} 9...Bg7 10.Qe3 {Once it does... We'll place a pawn in the center with tempo.} 10...d5 11.Nc3 {The most natural retreating square is c3. A very aggressive approach by us is trying to take advantage of the fact that White's c-pawn is blocked and chip away at their central structure.} 11...c5 12.dxc5 {White could take the free pawn...} 12...Nc6 {But we'll get the knight into the game, with prospects of ...e5 and ...d4 to get a ton of space. Suddenly, we are the one controlling the center! We'll castle short.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 2: The Tricky 2.Nc3 and 2.Bg5 Setups / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/3938883d-56f6-4a5a-a1da-d3a6daa5a3e1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of our side lines, we'll take a look at White playing Nc3 on move 2 instead of c4, trying to go for an early e4 or g4. Another possibility is the immediate Bg5, looking to capture our knight on f6 when it arrives there. We need to be prepared for both!} 1...f5 2.Nc3 {Another move White could play is Nc3 here. Although blocking the c-pawn, the idea is to try and achieve an early e4 or g4, as you'll see. We'll continue in our usual style.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {Our knight controls the e4-square. So, White often wants to get rid of it! Let's open up the bishop. Although we have to play this move in order to develop, it gives White the chance to push e4 immediately.} 3...e6 4.g4 {A move played about 5% of the time is g4. White wants to make us relinquish control of e4 and place a pawn there. Capturing here is not recommended. Let's pin White's knight instead! We don't mind them taking.} 4...Bb4 5.gxf5 {And they probably will. I recommend attacking the bishop here rather than capturing.} 5...h6 6.Bxf6 {If White takes, we'll recapture with the queen and gambit a pawn but reach a very active and healthy position. Our queen is eyeing the f2-pawn behind the f5-pawn...} 6...Qxf6 7.fxe6 {If White gets greedy and captures, we have an inbetween move. Let's castle with pressure on f2. Having already weakened their kingside with g4, White has to defend against this as well.} 7...O-O 8.Nf3 {The most natural defense is Nf3.} 8...d5 {And we'll place a pawn in the center with ...d5. The e6-pawn isn't going anywhere; we'll capture it with the bishop. After that, getting the knight into the game, probably via...Nd7, would be great.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 2: The Tricky 2.Nc3 and 2.Bg5 Setups / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/3938883d-56f6-4a5a-a1da-d3a6daa5a3e1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of our side lines, we'll take a look at White playing Nc3 on move 2 instead of c4, trying to go for an early e4 or g4. Another possibility is the immediate Bg5, looking to capture our knight on f6 when it arrives there. We need to be prepared for both!} 1...f5 2.Nc3 {Another move White could play is Nc3 here. Although blocking the c-pawn, the idea is to try and achieve an early e4 or g4, as you'll see. We'll continue in our usual style.} 2...Nf6 3.Bg5 {Our knight controls the e4-square. So, White often wants to get rid of it! Let's open up the bishop. Although we have to play this move in order to develop, it gives White the chance to push e4 immediately.} 3...e6 4.g4 {A move played about 5% of the time is g4. White wants to make us relinquish control of e4 and place a pawn there. Capturing here is not recommended. Let's pin White's knight instead! We don't mind them taking.} 4...Bb4 5.gxf5 {And they probably will. I recommend attacking the bishop here rather than capturing.} 5...h6 6.Bh4 {If White doesn't bite and chooses to retreat... Then we'll capture, with a concrete idea in mind. If White isn't careful, we'll suddenly play ...g5 and ...f4 and trap their bishop! This is another idea of ...h6.} 6...exf5 7.e3 {7.e3 grabs control of the f4-square and disallows us from trapping the bishop. That's fine, we'll still double White's pawns and keep developing.} 7...Bxc3+ 8.bxc3 {Once they recapture...} 8...Nc6 {We'll get the knight to c6. Our main idea is to play ...d5, contest the center and open up the light-squared bishop. We'll also castle, of course.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 3: 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 move order / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/59ea727c-dcc5-49c2-8c00-0207bcee9ce2/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll take a look at some specific and rare lines that aren't usually played, but that you might encounter! Nevertheless, they might be dangerous, so it's good to be prepared for them. They are 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 by White.} 1...f5 2.Qd3 {A bizarre style of play is activating the queen immediately and pressuring the f5-pawn that way. This breaks some opening principles but it could be a strong surprise weapon. We have to know how to deal with it. The most natural here is ...d5, defending the pawn with the bishop and grabbing space in the center.} 2...d5 3.g4 {After playing Qd3, 3.g4 is the most popular move, played about 44% of the time, called the Manhattan gambit. White wants to put maximum pressure on our f5-pawn, and the natural continuations would be ...e6 or ...g6 to defend the pawn. But we'll go for ...e5, as we want to initiate some trades in the center and make it easier for our pieces to develop!} 3...e5 4.dxe5 {If White captures on e5, we'll go for that pawn straight away. Our knight develops to c6 and attacks the pawn. White can't really play f4 to defend, as that would weaken the h4-d1 diagonal too much. If White plays Bf4, ...g5 is a good move.} 4...Nc6 5.gxf5 {So, White will most likely get greedy and capture on f5. We'll go ahead and get back one of the pawns with tempo on the queen. From here on, White has a couple of options.} 5...Nxe5 6.Qe3 {Some players will pin your knight on e5, which definitely looks dangerous. The threat is f4, winning the knight. But we're good! We'll develop the bishop and defend the knight in the center.} 6...Bd6 7.Nf3 {A smarter move than f4 is Nf3, adding an attacker to our pinned knight. We'll unpin and defend. Now we are threatening to capture on f3 and simplify the position.} 7...Qe7 8.Nxe5 {So, White will probably capture themselves first.} 8...Bxe5 {Let's avoid a queen trade and recapture with the bishop. Next, we'll develop normally and castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 3: 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 move order / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/59ea727c-dcc5-49c2-8c00-0207bcee9ce2/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll take a look at some specific and rare lines that aren't usually played, but that you might encounter! Nevertheless, they might be dangerous, so it's good to be prepared for them. They are 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 by White.} 1...f5 2.Qd3 {A bizarre style of play is activating the queen immediately and pressuring the f5-pawn that way. This breaks some opening principles but it could be a strong surprise weapon. We have to know how to deal with it. The most natural here is ...d5, defending the pawn with the bishop and grabbing space in the center.} 2...d5 3.g4 {After playing Qd3, 3.g4 is the most popular move, played about 44% of the time, called the Manhattan gambit. White wants to put maximum pressure on our f5-pawn, and the natural continuations would be ...e6 or ...g6 to defend the pawn. But we'll go for ...e5, as we want to initiate some trades in the center and make it easier for our pieces to develop!} 3...e5 4.dxe5 {If White captures on e5, we'll go for that pawn straight away. Our knight develops to c6 and attacks the pawn. White can't really play f4 to defend, as that would weaken the h4-d1 diagonal too much. If White plays Bf4, ...g5 is a good move.} 4...Nc6 5.gxf5 {So, White will most likely get greedy and capture on f5. We'll go ahead and get back one of the pawns with tempo on the queen. From here on, White has a couple of options.} 5...Nxe5 6.Qe3 {Some players will pin your knight on e5, which definitely looks dangerous. The threat is f4, winning the knight. But we're good! We'll develop the bishop and defend the knight in the center.} 6...Bd6 7.f4 {If White goes for f4, this is already a mistake. Yes, our knight can't move, but remember what I said about the diagonal that opens if White moves the f-pawn? Let's give a check from the h4-square.} 7...Qh4+ 8.Kd1 {White moves the king out of the way. We are not done with creating threats, though. Let's attack the queen with d4. If White wants to keep the pin and plays Qe4, they jump right into ...Nf6.} 8...d4 9.Qxd4 {So, White will most likely capture on d4. However, that allows for a great move by us.} 9...Ng4 {We'll move the knight to g4, with the threat of ...Nf2+. The best way for White to stop it is to play Nf3 followed by Qg1, which is very passive. Keep in mind that the f5-pawn is still hanging. Believe it or not, Black is winning here!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 3: 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 move order / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/59ea727c-dcc5-49c2-8c00-0207bcee9ce2/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll take a look at some specific and rare lines that aren't usually played, but that you might encounter! Nevertheless, they might be dangerous, so it's good to be prepared for them. They are 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 by White.} 1...f5 2.Qd3 {A bizarre style of play is activating the queen immediately and pressuring the f5-pawn that way. This breaks some opening principles but it could be a strong surprise weapon. We have to know how to deal with it. The most natural here is ...d5, defending the pawn with the bishop and grabbing space in the center.} 2...d5 3.g4 {After playing Qd3, 3.g4 is the most popular move, played about 44% of the time, called the Manhattan gambit. White wants to put maximum pressure on our f5-pawn, and the natural continuations would be ...e6 or ...g6 to defend the pawn. But we'll go for ...e5, as we want to initiate some trades in the center and make it easier for our pieces to develop!} 3...e5 4.gxf5 {Capturing on f5 doesn't pose us any problems whatsoever. We've accomplished placing two pawns in the center. Now, we'll disrupt White's center and make them move the queen once again, so that we can develop with tempo.} 4...exd4 5.Qxd4 {Many players will recapture here, as it is the most logical move. We'll activate the knight with tempo.} 5...Nc6 6.Qe3+ {If White retreats and gives a check... We'll block with the bishop. Our plan stays the same; activate the knight, capture on f5 and castle.} 6...Be7 7.Bh3 {White might try to hold on to the f5-pawn.} 7...Nf6 {But that's fine, we'll just get the knight out and castle on the next move. Our position is very strong with a lead in development, while White has played with the queen too many times and hasn't developed enough pieces! There is still chess to be played, however.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 3: 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 move order / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/59ea727c-dcc5-49c2-8c00-0207bcee9ce2/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll take a look at some specific and rare lines that aren't usually played, but that you might encounter! Nevertheless, they might be dangerous, so it's good to be prepared for them. They are 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 by White.} 1...f5 2.Qd3 {A bizarre style of play is activating the queen immediately and pressuring the f5-pawn that way. This breaks some opening principles but it could be a strong surprise weapon. We have to know how to deal with it. The most natural here is ...d5, defending the pawn with the bishop and grabbing space in the center.} 2...d5 3.g4 {After playing Qd3, 3.g4 is the most popular move, played about 44% of the time, called the Manhattan gambit. White wants to put maximum pressure on our f5-pawn, and the natural continuations would be ...e6 or ...g6 to defend the pawn. But we'll go for ...e5, as we want to initiate some trades in the center and make it easier for our pieces to develop!} 3...e5 4.dxe5 {If White captures on e5, we'll go for that pawn straight away. Our knight develops to c6 and attacks the pawn. White can't really play f4 to defend, as that would weaken the h4-d1 diagonal too much. If White plays Bf4, ...g5 is a good move.} 4...Nc6 5.gxf5 {So, White will most likely get greedy and capture on f5. We'll go ahead and get back one of the pawns with tempo on the queen. From here on, White has a couple of options.} 5...Nxe5 6.Qg3 {White might go Qg3 here, putting pressure on the knight while leaving space for the e-pawn to move. Also, we can't play ...Bd6, as that would hang our g7-pawn! I know the f7-square isn't really where the knight finds itself in most positions, but we are completely fine.} 6...Nf7 7.Nc3 {White can't threaten anything anymore, so they'll probably keep developing. Having moved the g-pawn, it's smarter for them to get the queenside pieces out and castle long quickly. Let's keep activating our own pieces.} 7...Nf6 8.Bd2 {Developing the bishop anywhere but d2 might be risky. This allows us to get the bishop out with tempo, though! You might notice that our g7-pawn is hanging. Let's see what happens if White blunders and takes it.} 8...Bd6 9.Qxg7 {If White is greedy, thinking they've won a pawn...} 9...Rg8 {Oops! Their queen suddenly gets trapped on the g7-square, with no squares to retreat to. We'll win the queen on the next move, and soon the game.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 3: 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 move order / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/59ea727c-dcc5-49c2-8c00-0207bcee9ce2/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll take a look at some specific and rare lines that aren't usually played, but that you might encounter! Nevertheless, they might be dangerous, so it's good to be prepared for them. They are 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 by White.} 1...f5 2.g4 {The peculiar-looking 2.g4 by White is already challenging the f5-pawn we just placed in the center. The idea is e4. This move is only played about 1% of the time in the Masters database. If we capture now, White will get e4, so we'll take the opportunity to play ...d5 and grab more control of the center.} 2...d5 3.g5 {If White goes for g5, they've just moved the g-pawn two times in the opening. Yes, we can't develop our knight to f6, but we'll get a lead in development. Let's challenge the center right away with ...c5. If White captures, we'll get to play ...e5.} 3...c5 4.c3 {So they'll most likely defend the pawn with c3. But we'll continue piling pressure. The knight on c6 is another attacker on the d4-pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {The most logical is for White to activate the knight and defend the pawn.} 5...e6 {We'll continue with ...e6, strengthening the center. I recommend activating the kingside knight to g6 via e7, that way it can pressure the bishop if it arrives on f4. Our bishop, on the other hand, will develop to the natural d6-square.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 3: 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 move order / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/59ea727c-dcc5-49c2-8c00-0207bcee9ce2/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll take a look at some specific and rare lines that aren't usually played, but that you might encounter! Nevertheless, they might be dangerous, so it's good to be prepared for them. They are 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 by White.} 1...f5 2.g4 {The peculiar-looking 2.g4 by White is already challenging the f5-pawn we just placed in the center. The idea is e4. This move is only played about 1% of the time in the Masters database. If we capture now, White will get e4, so we'll take the opportunity to play ...d5 and grab more control of the center.} 2...d5 3.gxf5 {If White captures on f5, they've just made our life easier. We'll simply recapture and develop a piece at the same time. We've got excellent control of the center.} 3...Bxf5 4.Bg2 {White might use the g-pawn moving to g4 and fianchetto the bishop... But we'll just keep developing. We've already got a lead in development with Black.} 4...Nf6 5.c4 {The most critical is for White to challenge the center with c4. If we capture, we'll lose the b7-pawn and the rook. So I recommend simply defending the center and letting White capture if they want to. Our bishop is also ready to get out.} 5...e6 6.Nc3 {If White develops the knight and adds another attacker to the center...} 6...Bb4 {We'll simply pin it. You've already seen this idea before. Next, we'll get our knight to c6 and castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 3: 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 move order / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/59ea727c-dcc5-49c2-8c00-0207bcee9ce2/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll take a look at some specific and rare lines that aren't usually played, but that you might encounter! Nevertheless, they might be dangerous, so it's good to be prepared for them. They are 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 by White.} 1...f5 2.Qd3 {A bizarre style of play is activating the queen immediately and pressuring the f5-pawn that way. This breaks some opening principles but it could be a strong surprise weapon. We have to know how to deal with it. The most natural here is ...d5, defending the pawn with the bishop and grabbing space in the center.} 2...d5 3.Nc3 {Instead of challenging the center with c4, White might try to put pressure on it with the knight on c3. The idea is to get the queenside pieces out fast and castle long. That just allows us to develop the knight to f6 and defend the center.} 3...Nf6 4.Bg5 {If White tries to pressure our knight, that's fine. We have more defenders! We'll play ...e6 and open up our dark-squared bishop. Pinning our knight is okay here as we are planning to play ...Be7 anyway.} 4...e6 5.O-O-O {White castles before activating the kingside...} 5...Be7 {And we'll unpin the knight. Our plan should be the following: After we castle, we'll play ...Nd7 and go for ...c5 to strike in the center! The idea is, that if the c-file opens up, we can use a rook in the future to attack on it. White's king is on c1, after all.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 3: 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 move order / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/59ea727c-dcc5-49c2-8c00-0207bcee9ce2/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll take a look at some specific and rare lines that aren't usually played, but that you might encounter! Nevertheless, they might be dangerous, so it's good to be prepared for them. They are 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 by White.} 1...f5 2.Qd3 {A bizarre style of play is activating the queen immediately and pressuring the f5-pawn that way. This breaks some opening principles but it could be a strong surprise weapon. We have to know how to deal with it. The most natural here is ...d5, defending the pawn with the bishop and grabbing space in the center.} 2...d5 3.c4 {Another possibility is to challenge the center right away with c4. But, as in the 2.g4 lines, we won't budge. We'll protect the center and open up the dark-squared bishop. In this line, I recommend the best approach to be turning our style of play into a Stonewall.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {White activates the knight to f3 and takes control of the e5-square. We'll do the same with our kingside knight, seizing control of the e4-square!} 4...Nf6 5.Nc3 {Both knights out. Before continuing development...} 5...Ne4 {Let's shove our knight into the center, Stonewall style! Next, we'll play ...c6 to solidify the center, get our bishop out and castle. Our position is very solid.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 3: 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 move order / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/59ea727c-dcc5-49c2-8c00-0207bcee9ce2/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we'll take a look at some specific and rare lines that aren't usually played, but that you might encounter! Nevertheless, they might be dangerous, so it's good to be prepared for them. They are 2.Qd3 and 2.g4 by White.} 1...f5 2.Qd3 {A bizarre style of play is activating the queen immediately and pressuring the f5-pawn that way. This breaks some opening principles but it could be a strong surprise weapon. We have to know how to deal with it. The most natural here is ...d5, defending the pawn with the bishop and grabbing space in the center.} 2...d5 3.g4 {After playing Qd3, 3.g4 is the most popular move, played about 44% of the time, called the Manhattan gambit. White wants to put maximum pressure on our f5-pawn, and the natural continuations would be ...e6 or ...g6 to defend the pawn. But we'll go for ...e5, as we want to initiate some trades in the center and make it easier for our pieces to develop!} 3...e5 4.gxf5 {Capturing on f5 doesn't pose us any problems whatsoever. We've accomplished placing two pawns in the center. Now, we'll disrupt White's center and make them move the queen once again, so that we can develop with tempo.} 4...exd4 5.Nf3 {Instead of capturing right away, White might want to develop and prepare to recapture with the knight. Well, we won't let them!} 5...c5 {We'll play ...c5 and hold on to the d4-pawn for now, creating a strong center. Next, we'll develop in a standard manner; get the knight to c6 to defend the center and try to capture on f5. If White plays Bh3 to defend the f5-pawn, we can go for ...Ne7 and attack it one more time!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 4: 2. e3 and 2. h3 move order / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/0b04bab0-309a-48e7-8b13-261bbafeaa33/lines"]

1.d4 {In the last study of this chapter, we'll look at the "really weird" second options for White, being e3 and h3. 2.e3 blocks the dark-squared bishop immediately, while 2.h3 goes against any opening principle we learn.} 1...f5 2.e3 {White decides to strengthen the center immediately. However, there is the obvious downside of blocking in the bishop on c1. We'll just play with this added benefit! Let's activate our knight behind the pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Bd3 {White usually plays e3 early in order to quickly develop the light-squared bishop and attack the pawn on f5. We won't go for ...g6 and ...Bg7 here. Instead, we'll defend the pawn with ...e6. This opens up our dark-squared bishop, and the other one will develop via ...b6 and ...Bb7.} 3...e6 4.c4 {The most common is for White to expand in the center. We'll continue with our plan, preparing the fianchetto.} 4...b6 5.Nc3 {White activates the knight to c3... And we'll go ahead and get the light-squared bishop out quickly. This forces White to react as the g2-pawn is under attack.} 5...Bb7 6.f3 {If they want to abstain from the Nf3-approach, they'll probably defend with f3. Let's strike in the center! If White captures, we'll gladly recapture with the bishop.} 6...c5 7.Nge2 {White activates the knight to e2, preparing to castle. Before we get the dark-squared bishop out, let's put more pressure on the center. We aren't afraid of losing tempi on the kingside, since our position is very solid.} 7...Nc6 8.O-O {White castles.} 8...Bd6 {And we'll get the last piece out. White has a dark-squared bishop problem that they'll probably try to fix with either b3 or e4. However, both of these moves take time. You can use this time to develop: ...Qc7 and ...O-O. The more challenging move, 9. d5, can be met with 9...Ne5. But at that point you should also expect to play ...Qc7 and ...O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 4: 2. e3 and 2. h3 move order / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/0b04bab0-309a-48e7-8b13-261bbafeaa33/lines"]

1.d4 {In the last study of this chapter, we'll look at the "really weird" second options for White, being e3 and h3. 2.e3 blocks the dark-squared bishop immediately, while 2.h3 goes against any opening principle we learn.} 1...f5 2.h3 {Another extremely peculiar approach by White is 2.h3. The idea is to support the g4-square and prepare g4. This shouldn't bother us. We'll continue developing naturally.} 2...Nf6 3.g4 {Once White plays g4, we'll stick to what we played before. We'll ignore their aggression and place a pawn in the center!} 3...d5 4.g5 {If they continue being aggressive and attack our knight... That's already a slight mistake. We'll shove the knight into the center and activate it that way. White still doesn't have any pieces out!} 4...Ne4 5.f3 {If White thinks they're smart and attack our knight again... They are going to get themselves into an uncomfortable position! Our knight jumps into g3 and attacks White's rook and the bishop.} 5...Ng3 6.Rh2 {Already, the rook has to move and White loses castling rights. Instead of capturing White's undeveloped bishop, let's grab the opportunity to strike in the center.} 6...c5 7.Bf4 {Finally, White develops a piece and attacks our knight. We'll jump to h5, counterattacking the bishop. Now White has to move it again.} 7...Nh5 8.Be5 {The most logical is Be5, centralizing the bishop. Anything else isn't as active.} 8...Nc6 {However, even after this move, their bishop becomes an easy target for our newly developed knight! In many cases, we'll be able to take on e5 favorably and then play ...e6 to target g5. There are many dark square weaknesses in this position for White that we'll exploit after getting rid of their e5-bishop—be on the lookout for strong moves with the queen on c7 or even b6. Trading and opening the position will benefit you.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Weird 2nd Moves / Study 4: 2. e3 and 2. h3 move order / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/ddb7d03c-bd4a-41da-bcee-a59e1edd6509/studies/0b04bab0-309a-48e7-8b13-261bbafeaa33/lines"]

1.d4 {In the last study of this chapter, we'll look at the "really weird" second options for White, being e3 and h3. 2.e3 blocks the dark-squared bishop immediately, while 2.h3 goes against any opening principle we learn.} 1...f5 2.h3 {Another extremely peculiar approach by White is 2.h3. The idea is to support the g4-square and prepare g4. This shouldn't bother us. We'll continue developing naturally.} 2...Nf6 3.g4 {Once White plays g4, we'll stick to what we played before. We'll ignore their aggression and place a pawn in the center!} 3...d5 4.g5 {If they continue being aggressive and attack our knight... That's already a slight mistake. We'll shove the knight into the center and activate it that way. White still doesn't have any pieces out!} 4...Ne4 5.h4 {5.h4 strengthens White's advanced structure on the kingside, but doesn't help them achieve any lead in development whatsoever. I recommend we continue to play in the center. Let's challenge the d4-pawn!} 5...c5 6.c3 {Capturing on c5 gives us all the central control, so c3, protecting the pawn, is more sensible.} 6...Nc6 {Let's just continue developing and add an attacker to the center. It's hard to suggest moves for White. If 7. g3, to kick the knight out, 7...Ng3 is strong. If 7. e3, you cannot go wrong with 7...e6 and normal development with ...Bd7, Qc7 and queenside castling.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Plays The Reti / Study 1: All Reti Lines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2d185019-8d7e-48d0-9c14-558824e22c9e/studies/8d6ec053-ec3e-4351-bcae-6c4bcfa477c7/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...f5 2.d3 {2. d3 is played with the idea of preparing the e4-pawn push. It occurs 8% of the times in the database, and has been played more than 100,000 times by players below 2000. Against this I suggest we just develop our queenside knight. A very tricky idea is that 2...Nf6 doesn't really stop 3. e4,as White can offer that pawn and have quick development.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White may see that we developed our knight into c6 and so they may attempt to bully us with the d-pawn! But going 2. d3 and 3. d4 is a silly way of trying to fight for an advantage. Here, we can control the d5-square a bit more.} 3...e6 4.c4 {4. c4 is an attempt to try and be aggressive about matters. I suggest we give a check here.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Bd2 {Some players will decide to block with the bishop. This is completely fine for us, as we'll deprive them of the bishop pair this way. Our bishop was looking to trade for something anyway. Let's take.} 5...Bxd2+ 6.Qxd2 {Recapturing with the queen is more logical if White is aiming to develop the knight to c3. Let's keep developing ourselves. Once again, we have a lot of control over the e4-square.} 6...Nf6 7.Nc3 {White disallows ...Ne4 for the moment, though, with Nc3. Let's castle for now, having completed kingside development.} 7...O-O 8.e3 {White will continue normally as well, opening up the light-squared bishop.} 8...d6 {And we'll go for ...d6. As in the previous lines, expect to fianchetto the light-squared bishop on b7. In the center, our main pawn push is still ...e5, and we'll prepare it with ...Qe7.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Plays The Reti / Study 1: All Reti Lines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2d185019-8d7e-48d0-9c14-558824e22c9e/studies/8d6ec053-ec3e-4351-bcae-6c4bcfa477c7/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...f5 2.d3 {2. d3 is played with the idea of preparing the e4-pawn push. It occurs 8% of the times in the database, and has been played more than 100,000 times by players below 2000. Against this I suggest we just develop our queenside knight. A very tricky idea is that 2...Nf6 doesn't really stop 3. e4,as White can offer that pawn and have quick development.} 2...Nc6 3.e4 {With a knight on c6 and not f6, it's easier to deal with 3. e4. We'll go for ...e5, not allowing White to grab too much space.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {Instead of capturing, Nc3 is a more sensible developing move. White takes more control of the d5-square and wants to make sure we can't play ...d5. As you've seen many times throughout all the Dutch lines, we often don't like the knight on c3 and pin it to the king.} 4...Bb4 5.exf5 {If White captures on f5 in this position too, you know what to do. Again, we'll place two pawns in the center. A threat here could even be ...d4.} 5...d5 6.a3 {That's why White might have to resolve the problems concerning our bishop now. Let's take and double their pawns. That was our plan anyway.} 6...Bxc3+ 7.bxc3 {White recaptures, and now the f5-pawn is hanging. We'll take and activate our bishop. Now, all the tensions in the center have been resolved. All that's left is for both sides to develop and castle.} 7...Bxf5 8.Be2 {White has played d3, so they have to accept the inferior Be2. We'll develop in a standard fashion, ready to castle.} 8...Nf6 9.O-O {When White castles...} 9...O-O {So will we. A good square for the queen would be d6, aiming to connect and activate the rooks.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Plays The Reti / Study 1: All Reti Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2d185019-8d7e-48d0-9c14-558824e22c9e/studies/8d6ec053-ec3e-4351-bcae-6c4bcfa477c7/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...f5 2.d3 {2. d3 is played with the idea of preparing the e4-pawn push. It occurs 8% of the times in the database, and has been played more than 100,000 times by players below 2000. Against this I suggest we just develop our queenside knight. A very tricky idea is that 2...Nf6 doesn't really stop 3. e4,as White can offer that pawn and have quick development.} 2...Nc6 3.e4 {With a knight on c6 and not f6, it's easier to deal with 3. e4. We'll go for ...e5, not allowing White to grab too much space.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {Instead of capturing, Nc3 is a more sensible developing move. White takes more control of the d5-square and wants to make sure we can't play ...d5. As you've seen many times throughout all the Dutch lines, we often don't like the knight on c3 and pin it to the king.} 4...Bb4 5.Bd2 {In this instance, if White defends with the bishop, it doesn't pay off to capture and let them have Bxc3. Instead, we'll just open up the light-squared bishop and solidify the center.} 5...d6 6.Nd5 {To relinquish the tension on the queenside, White will often jump to the center and offer a trade of bishops. Doing anything else but capturing here doesn't really make sense.} 6...Bxd2+ 7.Qxd2 {Once White recaptures...} 7...Nf6 {We'll get our knight to the center and challenge White's active one on d5. There's still tension in the center and we are ready to castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Plays The Reti / Study 1: All Reti Lines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2d185019-8d7e-48d0-9c14-558824e22c9e/studies/8d6ec053-ec3e-4351-bcae-6c4bcfa477c7/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...f5 2.d3 {2. d3 is played with the idea of preparing the e4-pawn push. It occurs 8% of the times in the database, and has been played more than 100,000 times by players below 2000. Against this I suggest we just develop our queenside knight. A very tricky idea is that 2...Nf6 doesn't really stop 3. e4,as White can offer that pawn and have quick development.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White may see that we developed our knight into c6 and so they may attempt to bully us with the d-pawn! But going 2. d3 and 3. d4 is a silly way of trying to fight for an advantage. Here, we can control the d5-square a bit more.} 3...e6 4.c4 {4. c4 is an attempt to try and be aggressive about matters. I suggest we give a check here.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Nc3 {Definitely, the most popular move is 5. Nc3, blocking the check with the knight. White argues, like in Chapter 1, that they don't mind doubled pawns. I've got to say they've got a point. A bishop pair is strong and it's compensation for having a weaker structure.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {White doubles their pawns.} 6...b6 {And this position is highly similar to Chapter 1. I know our knight is on c6, but it's actually not bad on that square. We'll develop all our pieces and be fine. If 7. d5 or d5 at some point, ...Na5 is a solid response. It opens up the bishop and places the knight on a safe square aiming at the queenside pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Plays The Reti / Study 1: All Reti Lines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2d185019-8d7e-48d0-9c14-558824e22c9e/studies/8d6ec053-ec3e-4351-bcae-6c4bcfa477c7/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...f5 2.d3 {2. d3 is played with the idea of preparing the e4-pawn push. It occurs 8% of the times in the database, and has been played more than 100,000 times by players below 2000. Against this I suggest we just develop our queenside knight. A very tricky idea is that 2...Nf6 doesn't really stop 3. e4,as White can offer that pawn and have quick development.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White may see that we developed our knight into c6 and so they may attempt to bully us with the d-pawn! But going 2. d3 and 3. d4 is a silly way of trying to fight for an advantage. Here, we can control the d5-square a bit more.} 3...e6 4.c4 {4. c4 is an attempt to try and be aggressive about matters. I suggest we give a check here.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Nbd2 {Some players are afraid of doubled pawns so they may block with their knight on d2. But this blocks the bishop in. The knight wants to go to c3 in these positions, not d2. We can just develop normally for now.} 5...Nf6 6.a3 {If we are prompted, we should take. This trade is good because our bishop didn't really have a future, and it clears the e4-square.} 6...Bxd2+ 7.Bxd2 {Once White recaptures, we don't want to go for attacks right away. We'll go for development.} 7...b6 {So definitely continue getting ready to fianchetto like in the Queen's Bishop Dutch Chapters. Since the knight is on c6, ...Ne7 to open the bishop up will be a good move.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Plays The Reti / Study 1: All Reti Lines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2d185019-8d7e-48d0-9c14-558824e22c9e/studies/8d6ec053-ec3e-4351-bcae-6c4bcfa477c7/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...f5 2.d3 {2. d3 is played with the idea of preparing the e4-pawn push. It occurs 8% of the times in the database, and has been played more than 100,000 times by players below 2000. Against this I suggest we just develop our queenside knight. A very tricky idea is that 2...Nf6 doesn't really stop 3. e4,as White can offer that pawn and have quick development.} 2...Nc6 3.d4 {White may see that we developed our knight into c6 and so they may attempt to bully us with the d-pawn! But going 2. d3 and 3. d4 is a silly way of trying to fight for an advantage. Here, we can control the d5-square a bit more.} 3...e6 4.g3 {And now if moves like g3 to fianchetto, this is a clear indication that White isn't going for anything aggressive. We now develop our knight to control the light squares even more.} 4...Nf6 5.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. And I like 5...d5 now, controlling the center and stopping the d-pawn on d4. We also get a grip on the e4-square, which we can use for a knight.} 5...d5 6.O-O {White usually castles.} 6...Bd6 {And we can place our bishop on d6. Expect to see moves like c4 at some point. You, however, can continue with castling and ...h6. White's pressure on d5 isn't enough so you can afford to develop your pieces normally.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Plays The Reti / Study 1: All Reti Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2d185019-8d7e-48d0-9c14-558824e22c9e/studies/8d6ec053-ec3e-4351-bcae-6c4bcfa477c7/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...f5 2.d3 {2. d3 is played with the idea of preparing the e4-pawn push. It occurs 8% of the times in the database, and has been played more than 100,000 times by players below 2000. Against this I suggest we just develop our queenside knight. A very tricky idea is that 2...Nf6 doesn't really stop 3. e4,as White can offer that pawn and have quick development.} 2...Nc6 3.e4 {With a knight on c6 and not f6, it's easier to deal with 3. e4. We'll go for ...e5, not allowing White to grab too much space.} 3...e5 4.exf5 {Capturing on f5 doesn't grant White any advantage whatsoever. If anything, it only gives us a crucial central square.} 4...d5 {We'll go ...d5, now with two strong pawns in the center. White's plans to achieve advantage have definitely backfired!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Plays The Reti / Study 1: All Reti Lines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2d185019-8d7e-48d0-9c14-558824e22c9e/studies/8d6ec053-ec3e-4351-bcae-6c4bcfa477c7/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...f5 2.e4 {An aggressive approach by White is the immediate e4. The idea is to gambit the central pawn and then try to recapture it or attack our king with Ng5. We'll accept the sacrifice. We aren't afraid of a gambit!} 2...fxe4 3.Ng5 {Ng5 attacks our pawn and prepares some Qh5+ in the future if we are not careful. For now, we'll just defend the e4-pawn, as Qh5+ is met with ...g6.} 3...d5 4.d3 {The most standard approach here is d3, offering to trade pawns on d3 but aiming to develop the bishop and have a lead in activity. Notice that we can't play ...h6 to attack the knight because of Qh5+. We won't bite. We'll stop Qh5+ by defending the g6-square and allowing us to play ...h6 now to kick out the knight.} 4...Qd6 5.dxe4 {If White captures in the center, we won't recapture and let them trade queens and centralize their knight. Instead, we'll attack the knight right away.} 5...h6 6.Qh5+ {If White goes for Qh5+, they just run into another blockade. As planned, we'll play ...g6 and attack the queen, defended by our own queen.} 6...g6 7.Qh4 {White has to play Qh4 here. We can't capture the knight right away because our rook on h8 would be hanging. But we'll just defend the rook with ...Bg7. Now the threat of capturing the knight is in the cards again.} 7...Bg7 8.Nf3 {So, White has to retreat. We've neutralized all of White's threats, so we can afford to continue developing here.} 8...Nf6 9.e5 {If White thinks we've blundered and tries to fork our pieces here...} 9...Qe6 {We'll pin the pawn to their king and save the knight that way. Next, we'll kick out their queen with ...g5 and a very comfortable position.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Plays The Reti / Study 1: All Reti Lines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2d185019-8d7e-48d0-9c14-558824e22c9e/studies/8d6ec053-ec3e-4351-bcae-6c4bcfa477c7/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...f5 2.e4 {An aggressive approach by White is the immediate e4. The idea is to gambit the central pawn and then try to recapture it or attack our king with Ng5. We'll accept the sacrifice. We aren't afraid of a gambit!} 2...fxe4 3.Ng5 {Ng5 attacks our pawn and prepares some Qh5+ in the future if we are not careful. For now, we'll just defend the e4-pawn, as Qh5+ is met with ...g6.} 3...d5 4.d3 {The most standard approach here is d3, offering to trade pawns on d3 but aiming to develop the bishop and have a lead in activity. Notice that we can't play ...h6 to attack the knight because of Qh5+. We won't bite. We'll stop Qh5+ by defending the g6-square and allowing us to play ...h6 now to kick out the knight.} 4...Qd6 5.dxe4 {If White captures in the center, we won't recapture and let them trade queens and centralize their knight. Instead, we'll attack the knight right away.} 5...h6 6.Nf3 {If White retreats to f3, we've got an easy decision.} 6...dxe4 {We'll capture in the center, attacking the knight again. We don't mind a queen trade, as it would undouble our e-pawns and keep an advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Plays The Reti / Study 1: All Reti Lines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2d185019-8d7e-48d0-9c14-558824e22c9e/studies/8d6ec053-ec3e-4351-bcae-6c4bcfa477c7/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...f5 2.e4 {An aggressive approach by White is the immediate e4. The idea is to gambit the central pawn and then try to recapture it or attack our king with Ng5. We'll accept the sacrifice. We aren't afraid of a gambit!} 2...fxe4 3.Ng5 {Ng5 attacks our pawn and prepares some Qh5+ in the future if we are not careful. For now, we'll just defend the e4-pawn, as Qh5+ is met with ...g6.} 3...d5 4.d3 {The most standard approach here is d3, offering to trade pawns on d3 but aiming to develop the bishop and have a lead in activity. Notice that we can't play ...h6 to attack the knight because of Qh5+. We won't bite. We'll stop Qh5+ by defending the g6-square and allowing us to play ...h6 now to kick out the knight.} 4...Qd6 5.dxe4 {If White captures in the center, we won't recapture and let them trade queens and centralize their knight. Instead, we'll attack the knight right away.} 5...h6 6.Nh3 {White going for Nh3 is simply passive. The knights don't belong on the edge of the board!} 6...dxe4 {We'll take in the center. As I've explained in the 6.Nf3 line, we are okay with a queen trade.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Plays The Reti / Study 1: All Reti Lines / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2d185019-8d7e-48d0-9c14-558824e22c9e/studies/8d6ec053-ec3e-4351-bcae-6c4bcfa477c7/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...f5 2.e4 {An aggressive approach by White is the immediate e4. The idea is to gambit the central pawn and then try to recapture it or attack our king with Ng5. We'll accept the sacrifice. We aren't afraid of a gambit!} 2...fxe4 3.Ng5 {Ng5 attacks our pawn and prepares some Qh5+ in the future if we are not careful. For now, we'll just defend the e4-pawn, as Qh5+ is met with ...g6.} 3...d5 4.c4 {If White tries to be more aggressive and challenge our d5-pawn...} 4...e6 {We can still defend with ...e6. This move is great because we attack the knight on g5 as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: White Plays The Reti / Study 1: All Reti Lines / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/ca013bce-73db-47f5-afbf-3f889a25eef1/chapters/2d185019-8d7e-48d0-9c14-558824e22c9e/studies/8d6ec053-ec3e-4351-bcae-6c4bcfa477c7/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...f5 2.e4 {An aggressive approach by White is the immediate e4. The idea is to gambit the central pawn and then try to recapture it or attack our king with Ng5. We'll accept the sacrifice. We aren't afraid of a gambit!} 2...fxe4 3.Ne5 {If White jumps to the center, it seems like they don't really know what they're doing. The threat is Qh5+.} 3...Nf6 {But we can easily stop it with ...Nf6, and will go for ...d6 on the next move to kick out the knight. White didn't make any effort to recapture our pawn, and we'll develop with ease, so we definitely have the advantage here!} *